window.CET6_BANKS.push({"id": "2017-12-1", "source": "2017年12月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: For this part, you are allowed 30 minutes to write an essay commenting on the saying \"Respect others, and you will be respected. \" you can cite examples to illustrate your views. You should write at least 150 words but no more than 200 words.", "sample": "The saying \"Respect others, and you will be respected\" concisely captures a truth about human relationships:\nrespect is not a one-way gift but a mutual exchange. I firmly agree that only by treating others with courtesy\ncan we earn their goodwill in return.\nA telling example is my former teammate, Li Ming. When our group project stalled, he never dismissed\nanyone's ideas; instead, he listened patiently and acknowledged every contribution. As a result, the whole team\ntrusted him and willingly followed his lead. His respect for others was repaid with the cooperation he received.\nHad he been arrogant and rude, the team would surely have turned against him.\nThis case reveals a simple yet powerful chain: respect given is respect returned. Since dignity is mutual, we\nshould not wait passively to be respected, but take the first step to value those around us. Once we do, a warm\ncycle of mutual respect will naturally take shape."}, "translation": {"prompt": "太湖是中国东部的一个淡水湖，占地面积2250平方公里，是中国第三大淡水湖，仅次于鄱阳和洞庭。太湖约有90个岛屿，大小从几平方米到几平方公里不等。太湖以其独特的\"太湖石\"而闻名，太湖石常用于装饰中国传统园林。太湖也以高产的捕鱼业闻名。自上世纪70年代后期以来，捕捞鱼蟹对沿湖的居民来说极为重要，并对周边地区的经济作出了重大贡献。太湖地区是中国陶瓷( ceramics)业基地之一，其中宜兴的陶瓷厂家生产举世闻名的宜兴紫砂壶( clay teapot)。", "sample": "Lake Taihu, a freshwater lake in eastern China covering an area of 2,250 square kilometers, is the third largest freshwater lake in China, after Poyang and Dongting. It has about 90 islands, ranging in size from a few square meters to several square kilometers. Lake Taihu is famous for its unique Taihu stones, which are often used to decorate traditional Chinese gardens. It is also famous for its productive fishing industry. Since the late 1970s, catching fish and crabs has been extremely important to the residents along the lake and has made significant contributions to the economy of the surrounding areas. The Taihu area is one of the ceramics bases in China, where the ceramics manufacturers in Yixing produce the world-famous Yixing clay teapots."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet6/paper/2017-12-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "In the past 12 months, Nigeria has suffered from a shrinking economy, a sliding currency, and a prolonged fuel shortage. Now, Africa's largest economy is facing a food crisis as major tomato fields have been destroyed by an insect, leading to a nationwide shortage and escalating prices.\nThe insect, Tutaabsoluta, has destroyed 80% of farms in Kaduna, Nigeria's largest tomato producing state, leading the government there to declare a state of  __26__  . The insect, also known as the tomato leaf miner, devastates crops by  __27__  on fruits and digging into and moving through stalks. It  __28__  incredibly quickly, breeding up to 12 generations per year if conditions are favorable. It is believed to have  __29__  in South America in the early 1900s, and later spread to Europe before crossing over to sub-Saharan Africa.\nIn Nigeria, where tomatoes are a staple of local diets, the insect's effects are devastating. Retail prices for a  __30__  of tomatoes at local markets have risen from $0.50 to $2.50. Farmers are reporting steep losses and a new $20 million tomato-paste factory has  __31__  production due to the shortages.\nGiven the moth's ability also to attack crops like pepper and potatoes, Audu Ogbeh, Nigeria's minister of agriculture, has warned that the pest may \"create serious problems for food  __32__  \" in the country. Ogbeh says experts are investigating how to control the pest's damage and prevent its spread, which has gone largely  __33__  until now.\nDespite being the continent's second-largest producer of tomatoes, Nigeria is  __34__  on $1 billion worth of tomato-paste imports every year, as around 75% of the local harvest goes to waste thanks to a lack of proper storage facilities. A further  __35__  in local supplies is yet another unwelcome setback to the industry.", "matchIntro": "Who's really addicting you to Technology?", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "\"Nearly everyone I know is addicted in some measure to the internet,\" wrote Tony Schwartz in The New York Times. It's a common complaint these days. A steady stream of similar headlines accuses the Net and its offspring apps, social media sites and online games of addicting us to distraction."}, {"label": "B", "text": "There's little doubt that nearly everyone who comes in contact with the Net has difficulty disconnecting. Many of us, like Schwartz, struggle to stay focused on tasks that require more concentration than it takes to post a status update. As one person ironically put it in the comments section of Schwartz's online article, \"As I was reading this very excellent article, I stopped at least half a dozen times to check my email.\""}, {"label": "C", "text": "There's something different about this technology: it is both invasive and persuasive. But who's at fault for its overuse? To find solutions, it's important to understand what we're dealing with. There are four parties conspiring to keep you connected: the tech, your boss, your friends and you."}, {"label": "D", "text": "The technologies themselves and their makers are the easiest suspects to blame for our diminishing attention spans. Nicholas Carr, author of The Shallows: what the internet is doing to our brains, wrote, \"The Net is designed to be an interruption system, a machine geared to dividing attention.\""}, {"label": "E", "text": "Online services like Facebook, Twitter and the like, are called out as masters of manipulation—making products so good that people can't stop using them. After studying these products for several years, I wrote a book about how they do it. I learned it all starts with the business model. Since these services rely on advertising revenue, the more frequently you use them, the more money they make. It's no wonder these companies employ teams of people focused on engineering their services to be as engaging as possible. These products aren't habit-forming by chance; it's by design. They have an incentive to keep us hooked."}, {"label": "F", "text": "However, as good as these services are, there are simple steps we can take to keep them at bay. For example, we can change how often we receive the distracting notifications that trigger our urge to check. According to Adam Marchick, CEO of mobile marketing company Kahuna, less than 15 percent of smartphone users ever bother to adjust their notification settings—meaning the remaining 85 percent of us default to the app makers' every preset trigger. Google and Apple have made it far too difficult to adjust these settings so it's up to us to take steps to ensure we set these triggers to suit our own needs, not the needs of the app makers."}, {"label": "G", "text": "While companies like Facebook harvest attention to generate revenue from advertisers, other technologies have no such agenda. Take email, for example. This system couldn't care less how often you use it. Yet to many, email is the most habit-forming medium of all. We check email at all hours of the day—we're obsessed. But why? Because that's what the boss wants. For almost all white-collar jobs, email is the primary tool of corporate communication. A slow response to a message could hurt not only your reputation but also your livelihood."}, {"label": "H", "text": "Your friends are also responsible. Think about this familiar scene. People gathered around a table, enjoying food and each other's company. There's laughter and a bit of kidding. Then, during an interval in the conversation, someone takes out their phone to check who knows what. Barely anyone notices and no one says a thing."}, {"label": "I", "text": "Now imagine the same dinner, but instead of checking their phone, the person belches(打嗝) loudly. Everyone notices. Unless the meal takes place in a beer house, this is considered bad manners. The impolite act violates the basic rules of etiquette. One has to wonder: why don't we apply the same social norms to checking phones during meals, meetings and conversations as we do to other antisocial behaviors? Somehow, we accept it and say nothing when someone offends."}, {"label": "J", "text": "The reality is taking one's phone out at the wrong time is worse than belching because, unlike other minor offenses, checking tech is contagious. Once one person looks at their phone, other people feel compelled to do the same, starting a chain reaction. The more people are on their phones, the fewer people are talking until finally you are the only one left not reading email or checking Twitter. From a societal perspective, phone checking is less like belching in public and more like another bad habit. Our phones are like cigarettes—something to do when we're anxious, bored or when our fingers need something to toy with. Seeing others enjoy a smoke, or sneak a quick glance, is too tempting to resist and soon everyone is doing it."}, {"label": "K", "text": "The technology, your boss, and your friends, all influence how often you find yourself using (or overusing) these gadgets. But there's still someone who deserves scrutiny—the person holding the phone."}, {"label": "L", "text": "I have a confession. Even though I study habit-forming technology for a living, disconnecting is not easy for me. I'm online far more than I'd like. Like Schwartz and so many others, I often find myself distracted and off task. I wanted to know why so I began self-monitoring to try to understand my behavior. That's when I discovered an uncomfortable truth. I use technology as an escape. When I'm doing something I'd rather not do, or when I'm someplace I'd rather not be, I use my phone to port myself elsewhere. I found that this ability to instantly shift my attention was often a good thing, like when passing time on public transportation. But frequently my tech use was not so benign. When I faced difficult work, like thinking through an article idea or editing the same draft for the hundredth time, for example, a more sinister screen would draw me in. I could easily escape discomfort, temporarily, by answering emails or browsing the web under the pretense of so-called \"research.\" Though I desperately wanted to lay blame elsewhere, I finally had to admit that my bad habits had less to do with new-age technology and more to do with old-fashioned procrastination(拖延)."}, {"label": "M", "text": "It's easy to blame technology for being so distracting, but distraction is nothing new. Aristotle and Socrates debated nature of \"akrasia\"—our tendency to do things against our interests. If we're honest with ourselves, tech is just another way to occupy our time and minds. If we weren't on our devices, we'd likely do something similarly unproductive."}, {"label": "N", "text": "Personal technology is indeed more engaging than ever, and there's no doubt companies are engineering their products and services to be more compelling and attractive. But would we want it any other way? The intended result of making something better is that people use it more. That's not necessarily a problem, that's progress."}, {"label": "O", "text": "These improvements don't mean we shouldn't attempt to control our use of technology. In order to make sure it doesn't control us, we should come to terms with the fact that it's more than the technology itself that's responsible for our habits. Our workplace culture, social norms and individual behaviors all play a part. To put technology in its place, we must be conscious not only of how technology is changing, but also of how it is changing us."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "You may have heard that Coca-Cola once contained an ingredient capable of sparking particular devotion in consumers: cocaine. The \"Coca\" in the name referred to the extracts of coca leaf that the drink's originator, chemist John Pemberton, mixed with his sugary syrup(浆汁). At the time, coca leaf extract mixed with wine was a common tonic(滋补品), and Pemberton's sweet brew was a way to get around local laws prohibiting the sale of alcohol. But the other half of the name presents another ingredient, less infamous(名声不好的), perhaps, but also strangely potent: the kola nut.\nIn West Africa, people have long chewed kola nuts as stimulants, because they contain caffeine that also occurs naturally in tea, coffee, and chocolate. They also have heart stimulants.\nHistorian Paul Lovejoy relates that the cultivation of kola nuts in West Africa is hundreds of years old. The leafy, spreading trees were planted on graves and as part of traditional rituals. Even though the nuts, which need to stay moist, can be somewhat delicate to transport, traders carried them hundreds of miles throughout the forests and grasslands.\nEuropeans did not know of them until the 1500s, when Portuguese ships arrived on the coast of what is now Sierra Leone. And while the Portuguese took part in the trade, ferrying nuts down the coast along with other goods, by 1620, when English explorer Richard Jobson made his way up the Gambia, the nuts were still peculiar to his eyes.\nBy the late 19th century, kola nuts were being shipped by the tonne to Europe and the US. Many made their way into medicines, intended as a kind of energy boost. One such popular medicinal drink was Vin Mariani, a French product consisting of coca extract mixed with red wine. It was created by a French chemist, Angelo Mariani, in 1863. So when Pemberton created his drink, it represented an ongoing trend. When cocaine eventually fell from grace as a beverage ingredient, kola-extract colas became popular.\nThe first year it was available, Coca-Cola averaged nine servings a day across all the Atlanta soda fountains where it was sold. As it grew more popular, the company sold rights to bottle the soda, so it could travel easily. Today about 1.9 billion Cokes are purchased daily. It's become so iconic that attempts to change its taste in 1985—sweetening it in a move projected to boost sales—proved disastrous, with widespread anger from consumers. \"Coca-Cola Classic\" returned to store shelves just three months after the \"New Coke\" was released.\nThese days, the Coca-Cola recipe is a closely guarded secret. But it's said to no longer contain kola nut extract, relying instead on artificial imitations to achieve the flavour.", "47": "You may have heard that Coca-Cola once contained an ingredient capable of sparking particular devotion in consumers: cocaine. The \"Coca\" in the name referred to the extracts of coca leaf that the drink's originator, chemist John Pemberton, mixed with his sugary syrup(浆汁). At the time, coca leaf extract mixed with wine was a common tonic(滋补品), and Pemberton's sweet brew was a way to get around local laws prohibiting the sale of alcohol. But the other half of the name presents another ingredient, less infamous(名声不好的), perhaps, but also strangely potent: the kola nut.\nIn West Africa, people have long chewed kola nuts as stimulants, because they contain caffeine that also occurs naturally in tea, coffee, and chocolate. They also have heart stimulants.\nHistorian Paul Lovejoy relates that the cultivation of kola nuts in West Africa is hundreds of years old. The leafy, spreading trees were planted on graves and as part of traditional rituals. Even though the nuts, which need to stay moist, can be somewhat delicate to transport, traders carried them hundreds of miles throughout the forests and grasslands.\nEuropeans did not know of them until the 1500s, when Portuguese ships arrived on the coast of what is now Sierra Leone. And while the Portuguese took part in the trade, ferrying nuts down the coast along with other goods, by 1620, when English explorer Richard Jobson made his way up the Gambia, the nuts were still peculiar to his eyes.\nBy the late 19th century, kola nuts were being shipped by the tonne to Europe and the US. Many made their way into medicines, intended as a kind of energy boost. One such popular medicinal drink was Vin Mariani, a French product consisting of coca extract mixed with red wine. It was created by a French chemist, Angelo Mariani, in 1863. So when Pemberton created his drink, it represented an ongoing trend. When cocaine eventually fell from grace as a beverage ingredient, kola-extract colas became popular.\nThe first year it was available, Coca-Cola averaged nine servings a day across all the Atlanta soda fountains where it was sold. As it grew more popular, the company sold rights to bottle the soda, so it could travel easily. Today about 1.9 billion Cokes are purchased daily. It's become so iconic that attempts to change its taste in 1985—sweetening it in a move projected to boost sales—proved disastrous, with widespread anger from consumers. \"Coca-Cola Classic\" returned to store shelves just three months after the \"New Coke\" was released.\nThese days, the Coca-Cola recipe is a closely guarded secret. But it's said to no longer contain kola nut extract, relying instead on artificial imitations to achieve the flavour.", "48": "You may have heard that Coca-Cola once contained an ingredient capable of sparking particular devotion in consumers: cocaine. The \"Coca\" in the name referred to the extracts of coca leaf that the drink's originator, chemist John Pemberton, mixed with his sugary syrup(浆汁). At the time, coca leaf extract mixed with wine was a common tonic(滋补品), and Pemberton's sweet brew was a way to get around local laws prohibiting the sale of alcohol. But the other half of the name presents another ingredient, less infamous(名声不好的), perhaps, but also strangely potent: the kola nut.\nIn West Africa, people have long chewed kola nuts as stimulants, because they contain caffeine that also occurs naturally in tea, coffee, and chocolate. They also have heart stimulants.\nHistorian Paul Lovejoy relates that the cultivation of kola nuts in West Africa is hundreds of years old. The leafy, spreading trees were planted on graves and as part of traditional rituals. Even though the nuts, which need to stay moist, can be somewhat delicate to transport, traders carried them hundreds of miles throughout the forests and grasslands.\nEuropeans did not know of them until the 1500s, when Portuguese ships arrived on the coast of what is now Sierra Leone. And while the Portuguese took part in the trade, ferrying nuts down the coast along with other goods, by 1620, when English explorer Richard Jobson made his way up the Gambia, the nuts were still peculiar to his eyes.\nBy the late 19th century, kola nuts were being shipped by the tonne to Europe and the US. Many made their way into medicines, intended as a kind of energy boost. One such popular medicinal drink was Vin Mariani, a French product consisting of coca extract mixed with red wine. It was created by a French chemist, Angelo Mariani, in 1863. So when Pemberton created his drink, it represented an ongoing trend. When cocaine eventually fell from grace as a beverage ingredient, kola-extract colas became popular.\nThe first year it was available, Coca-Cola averaged nine servings a day across all the Atlanta soda fountains where it was sold. As it grew more popular, the company sold rights to bottle the soda, so it could travel easily. Today about 1.9 billion Cokes are purchased daily. It's become so iconic that attempts to change its taste in 1985—sweetening it in a move projected to boost sales—proved disastrous, with widespread anger from consumers. \"Coca-Cola Classic\" returned to store shelves just three months after the \"New Coke\" was released.\nThese days, the Coca-Cola recipe is a closely guarded secret. But it's said to no longer contain kola nut extract, relying instead on artificial imitations to achieve the flavour.", "49": "You may have heard that Coca-Cola once contained an ingredient capable of sparking particular devotion in consumers: cocaine. The \"Coca\" in the name referred to the extracts of coca leaf that the drink's originator, chemist John Pemberton, mixed with his sugary syrup(浆汁). At the time, coca leaf extract mixed with wine was a common tonic(滋补品), and Pemberton's sweet brew was a way to get around local laws prohibiting the sale of alcohol. But the other half of the name presents another ingredient, less infamous(名声不好的), perhaps, but also strangely potent: the kola nut.\nIn West Africa, people have long chewed kola nuts as stimulants, because they contain caffeine that also occurs naturally in tea, coffee, and chocolate. They also have heart stimulants.\nHistorian Paul Lovejoy relates that the cultivation of kola nuts in West Africa is hundreds of years old. The leafy, spreading trees were planted on graves and as part of traditional rituals. Even though the nuts, which need to stay moist, can be somewhat delicate to transport, traders carried them hundreds of miles throughout the forests and grasslands.\nEuropeans did not know of them until the 1500s, when Portuguese ships arrived on the coast of what is now Sierra Leone. And while the Portuguese took part in the trade, ferrying nuts down the coast along with other goods, by 1620, when English explorer Richard Jobson made his way up the Gambia, the nuts were still peculiar to his eyes.\nBy the late 19th century, kola nuts were being shipped by the tonne to Europe and the US. Many made their way into medicines, intended as a kind of energy boost. One such popular medicinal drink was Vin Mariani, a French product consisting of coca extract mixed with red wine. It was created by a French chemist, Angelo Mariani, in 1863. So when Pemberton created his drink, it represented an ongoing trend. When cocaine eventually fell from grace as a beverage ingredient, kola-extract colas became popular.\nThe first year it was available, Coca-Cola averaged nine servings a day across all the Atlanta soda fountains where it was sold. As it grew more popular, the company sold rights to bottle the soda, so it could travel easily. Today about 1.9 billion Cokes are purchased daily. It's become so iconic that attempts to change its taste in 1985—sweetening it in a move projected to boost sales—proved disastrous, with widespread anger from consumers. \"Coca-Cola Classic\" returned to store shelves just three months after the \"New Coke\" was released.\nThese days, the Coca-Cola recipe is a closely guarded secret. But it's said to no longer contain kola nut extract, relying instead on artificial imitations to achieve the flavour.", "50": "You may have heard that Coca-Cola once contained an ingredient capable of sparking particular devotion in consumers: cocaine. The \"Coca\" in the name referred to the extracts of coca leaf that the drink's originator, chemist John Pemberton, mixed with his sugary syrup(浆汁). At the time, coca leaf extract mixed with wine was a common tonic(滋补品), and Pemberton's sweet brew was a way to get around local laws prohibiting the sale of alcohol. But the other half of the name presents another ingredient, less infamous(名声不好的), perhaps, but also strangely potent: the kola nut.\nIn West Africa, people have long chewed kola nuts as stimulants, because they contain caffeine that also occurs naturally in tea, coffee, and chocolate. They also have heart stimulants.\nHistorian Paul Lovejoy relates that the cultivation of kola nuts in West Africa is hundreds of years old. The leafy, spreading trees were planted on graves and as part of traditional rituals. Even though the nuts, which need to stay moist, can be somewhat delicate to transport, traders carried them hundreds of miles throughout the forests and grasslands.\nEuropeans did not know of them until the 1500s, when Portuguese ships arrived on the coast of what is now Sierra Leone. And while the Portuguese took part in the trade, ferrying nuts down the coast along with other goods, by 1620, when English explorer Richard Jobson made his way up the Gambia, the nuts were still peculiar to his eyes.\nBy the late 19th century, kola nuts were being shipped by the tonne to Europe and the US. Many made their way into medicines, intended as a kind of energy boost. One such popular medicinal drink was Vin Mariani, a French product consisting of coca extract mixed with red wine. It was created by a French chemist, Angelo Mariani, in 1863. So when Pemberton created his drink, it represented an ongoing trend. When cocaine eventually fell from grace as a beverage ingredient, kola-extract colas became popular.\nThe first year it was available, Coca-Cola averaged nine servings a day across all the Atlanta soda fountains where it was sold. As it grew more popular, the company sold rights to bottle the soda, so it could travel easily. Today about 1.9 billion Cokes are purchased daily. It's become so iconic that attempts to change its taste in 1985—sweetening it in a move projected to boost sales—proved disastrous, with widespread anger from consumers. \"Coca-Cola Classic\" returned to store shelves just three months after the \"New Coke\" was released.\nThese days, the Coca-Cola recipe is a closely guarded secret. But it's said to no longer contain kola nut extract, relying instead on artificial imitations to achieve the flavour.", "51": "Twenty years ago, the Urban Land Institute defined the two types of cities that dominated the US landscape: smaller cities that operated around standard 9-5 business hours and large metropolitan areas that ran all 24 hours of the day. Analyzing and comparing cities using the lens of this basic divide gives interesting context to how investment capital flows and housing prices have shifted.\nIn recent years, many mid-sized cities have begun to adopt a middle-of-the-road approach incorporating the excitement and opportunity of large cities with small cities' quiet after midnight. These 18-hour cities are beginning to make waves in real estate rankings and attract more real estate investment. What is underlying this new movement in real estate, and why do these cities have so much appeal?\n18-hour cities combine the best of 24-hour and 9-5 cities, which contributes to downtown revitalization. For decades, many downtown cores in small to mid-sized cities were abandoned after work hours by workers who lived in the suburbs. Movement out of city centers was widespread, and downtown tenants were predominantly made up of the working poor. This generated little commerce for downtown businesses in the evenings, which made business and generating tax revenue for municipal upkeep difficult. With the rise of a new concept in urban planning that aims to make life easier and more convenient, however, increasing popularity for urban areas that caused the real estate pushes, in major cities like San Francisco or New York, has inspired a type of forward thinking urbanity and policy in smaller cities.\nTransforming downtown areas so that they incorporate modern housing and improved walkability to local restaurants, retail, and entertainment—especially when combined with improved infrastructure for cyclists and public transit—makes them appeal to a more affluent demographic. These adjustments encourage employers in the knowledge and talent industries to keep their offices downtown. Access to foot traffic and proximity to transit allow the type of entertainment-oriented businesses such as bars and restaurants to stay open later, which attracts both younger, creative workers and baby boomers nearing retirement alike. Because of their smaller size, most keep hours that allow people to enjoy themselves, then have some quiet after midnight, as opposed to large major cities like New York, where the buzz of activity is ongoing.\nThese 18-hour cities are rapidly on the rise and offer great opportunities for homeowner investment. In many of these cities such as Denver, a diverse and vigorous economy attracted to the urban core has offered stable employment for residents. The right urban mix has propped up home occupancy, increased property values, and attracted significant investment capital.", "52": "Twenty years ago, the Urban Land Institute defined the two types of cities that dominated the US landscape: smaller cities that operated around standard 9-5 business hours and large metropolitan areas that ran all 24 hours of the day. Analyzing and comparing cities using the lens of this basic divide gives interesting context to how investment capital flows and housing prices have shifted.\nIn recent years, many mid-sized cities have begun to adopt a middle-of-the-road approach incorporating the excitement and opportunity of large cities with small cities' quiet after midnight. These 18-hour cities are beginning to make waves in real estate rankings and attract more real estate investment. What is underlying this new movement in real estate, and why do these cities have so much appeal?\n18-hour cities combine the best of 24-hour and 9-5 cities, which contributes to downtown revitalization. For decades, many downtown cores in small to mid-sized cities were abandoned after work hours by workers who lived in the suburbs. Movement out of city centers was widespread, and downtown tenants were predominantly made up of the working poor. This generated little commerce for downtown businesses in the evenings, which made business and generating tax revenue for municipal upkeep difficult. With the rise of a new concept in urban planning that aims to make life easier and more convenient, however, increasing popularity for urban areas that caused the real estate pushes, in major cities like San Francisco or New York, has inspired a type of forward thinking urbanity and policy in smaller cities.\nTransforming downtown areas so that they incorporate modern housing and improved walkability to local restaurants, retail, and entertainment—especially when combined with improved infrastructure for cyclists and public transit—makes them appeal to a more affluent demographic. These adjustments encourage employers in the knowledge and talent industries to keep their offices downtown. Access to foot traffic and proximity to transit allow the type of entertainment-oriented businesses such as bars and restaurants to stay open later, which attracts both younger, creative workers and baby boomers nearing retirement alike. Because of their smaller size, most keep hours that allow people to enjoy themselves, then have some quiet after midnight, as opposed to large major cities like New York, where the buzz of activity is ongoing.\nThese 18-hour cities are rapidly on the rise and offer great opportunities for homeowner investment. In many of these cities such as Denver, a diverse and vigorous economy attracted to the urban core has offered stable employment for residents. The right urban mix has propped up home occupancy, increased property values, and attracted significant investment capital.", "53": "Twenty years ago, the Urban Land Institute defined the two types of cities that dominated the US landscape: smaller cities that operated around standard 9-5 business hours and large metropolitan areas that ran all 24 hours of the day. Analyzing and comparing cities using the lens of this basic divide gives interesting context to how investment capital flows and housing prices have shifted.\nIn recent years, many mid-sized cities have begun to adopt a middle-of-the-road approach incorporating the excitement and opportunity of large cities with small cities' quiet after midnight. These 18-hour cities are beginning to make waves in real estate rankings and attract more real estate investment. What is underlying this new movement in real estate, and why do these cities have so much appeal?\n18-hour cities combine the best of 24-hour and 9-5 cities, which contributes to downtown revitalization. For decades, many downtown cores in small to mid-sized cities were abandoned after work hours by workers who lived in the suburbs. Movement out of city centers was widespread, and downtown tenants were predominantly made up of the working poor. This generated little commerce for downtown businesses in the evenings, which made business and generating tax revenue for municipal upkeep difficult. With the rise of a new concept in urban planning that aims to make life easier and more convenient, however, increasing popularity for urban areas that caused the real estate pushes, in major cities like San Francisco or New York, has inspired a type of forward thinking urbanity and policy in smaller cities.\nTransforming downtown areas so that they incorporate modern housing and improved walkability to local restaurants, retail, and entertainment—especially when combined with improved infrastructure for cyclists and public transit—makes them appeal to a more affluent demographic. These adjustments encourage employers in the knowledge and talent industries to keep their offices downtown. Access to foot traffic and proximity to transit allow the type of entertainment-oriented businesses such as bars and restaurants to stay open later, which attracts both younger, creative workers and baby boomers nearing retirement alike. Because of their smaller size, most keep hours that allow people to enjoy themselves, then have some quiet after midnight, as opposed to large major cities like New York, where the buzz of activity is ongoing.\nThese 18-hour cities are rapidly on the rise and offer great opportunities for homeowner investment. In many of these cities such as Denver, a diverse and vigorous economy attracted to the urban core has offered stable employment for residents. The right urban mix has propped up home occupancy, increased property values, and attracted significant investment capital.", "54": "Twenty years ago, the Urban Land Institute defined the two types of cities that dominated the US landscape: smaller cities that operated around standard 9-5 business hours and large metropolitan areas that ran all 24 hours of the day. Analyzing and comparing cities using the lens of this basic divide gives interesting context to how investment capital flows and housing prices have shifted.\nIn recent years, many mid-sized cities have begun to adopt a middle-of-the-road approach incorporating the excitement and opportunity of large cities with small cities' quiet after midnight. These 18-hour cities are beginning to make waves in real estate rankings and attract more real estate investment. What is underlying this new movement in real estate, and why do these cities have so much appeal?\n18-hour cities combine the best of 24-hour and 9-5 cities, which contributes to downtown revitalization. For decades, many downtown cores in small to mid-sized cities were abandoned after work hours by workers who lived in the suburbs. Movement out of city centers was widespread, and downtown tenants were predominantly made up of the working poor. This generated little commerce for downtown businesses in the evenings, which made business and generating tax revenue for municipal upkeep difficult. With the rise of a new concept in urban planning that aims to make life easier and more convenient, however, increasing popularity for urban areas that caused the real estate pushes, in major cities like San Francisco or New York, has inspired a type of forward thinking urbanity and policy in smaller cities.\nTransforming downtown areas so that they incorporate modern housing and improved walkability to local restaurants, retail, and entertainment—especially when combined with improved infrastructure for cyclists and public transit—makes them appeal to a more affluent demographic. These adjustments encourage employers in the knowledge and talent industries to keep their offices downtown. Access to foot traffic and proximity to transit allow the type of entertainment-oriented businesses such as bars and restaurants to stay open later, which attracts both younger, creative workers and baby boomers nearing retirement alike. Because of their smaller size, most keep hours that allow people to enjoy themselves, then have some quiet after midnight, as opposed to large major cities like New York, where the buzz of activity is ongoing.\nThese 18-hour cities are rapidly on the rise and offer great opportunities for homeowner investment. In many of these cities such as Denver, a diverse and vigorous economy attracted to the urban core has offered stable employment for residents. The right urban mix has propped up home occupancy, increased property values, and attracted significant investment capital.", "55": "Twenty years ago, the Urban Land Institute defined the two types of cities that dominated the US landscape: smaller cities that operated around standard 9-5 business hours and large metropolitan areas that ran all 24 hours of the day. Analyzing and comparing cities using the lens of this basic divide gives interesting context to how investment capital flows and housing prices have shifted.\nIn recent years, many mid-sized cities have begun to adopt a middle-of-the-road approach incorporating the excitement and opportunity of large cities with small cities' quiet after midnight. These 18-hour cities are beginning to make waves in real estate rankings and attract more real estate investment. What is underlying this new movement in real estate, and why do these cities have so much appeal?\n18-hour cities combine the best of 24-hour and 9-5 cities, which contributes to downtown revitalization. For decades, many downtown cores in small to mid-sized cities were abandoned after work hours by workers who lived in the suburbs. Movement out of city centers was widespread, and downtown tenants were predominantly made up of the working poor. This generated little commerce for downtown businesses in the evenings, which made business and generating tax revenue for municipal upkeep difficult. With the rise of a new concept in urban planning that aims to make life easier and more convenient, however, increasing popularity for urban areas that caused the real estate pushes, in major cities like San Francisco or New York, has inspired a type of forward thinking urbanity and policy in smaller cities.\nTransforming downtown areas so that they incorporate modern housing and improved walkability to local restaurants, retail, and entertainment—especially when combined with improved infrastructure for cyclists and public transit—makes them appeal to a more affluent demographic. These adjustments encourage employers in the knowledge and talent industries to keep their offices downtown. Access to foot traffic and proximity to transit allow the type of entertainment-oriented businesses such as bars and restaurants to stay open later, which attracts both younger, creative workers and baby boomers nearing retirement alike. Because of their smaller size, most keep hours that allow people to enjoy themselves, then have some quiet after midnight, as opposed to large major cities like New York, where the buzz of activity is ongoing.\nThese 18-hour cities are rapidly on the rise and offer great opportunities for homeowner investment. In many of these cities such as Denver, a diverse and vigorous economy attracted to the urban core has offered stable employment for residents. The right urban mix has propped up home occupancy, increased property values, and attracted significant investment capital."}, "questions": [{"id": "2017-12-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What does the woman say about the new laws in Italy?", "options": ["A) They reward businesses that eliminate food waste.", "B) They prohibit the sale of foods that have gone stale.", "C) They facilitate the donation of unsold foods to the needy.", "D) They forbid businesses to produce more foods than needed."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） they facilitate the donation of unsold foods to the needy.\n【定位】New laws have been put into place that would make it easier for farms and supermarkets to\ndonate unsold foods to those who are in need.\n新法已经出台，将使农场和超市更容易把卖不掉的食物捐给那些有需要的人。\n【信号】女士直接介绍新法内容。这句出现在女士说 the Italian government passed legislation 之\n后，紧接着用 New laws have been put into place 展开新法的具体作用，是「谁说 + 说\n什么」直接落点，正对题干问的「女士关于新法说了什么」。\n【替换】make it easier for farms and supermarkets to donate unsold foods to those who are in need ↔\nthey facilitate the donation of unsold foods to the needy\nmake it easier = facilitate\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n「奖励减少浪费的商家」确实在录音里出现，但出自男士的 businesses will now be\nrewarded for successful efforts to cut food waste，而本题问的是「女士说」的新法内\n容，把男士补充的内容安到本题，属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：businesses will now be rewarded for successful efforts to cut food waste\nB) 未提及\n选项说禁止出售变质食品（gone stale），但女士介绍意大利新法时只谈捐赠，全程未提\n「禁止销售过期 / 变质食品」，属未提及的编造。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 forbid businesses to produce more foods than needed 借用了录音反复出现的\nfoods 一词，制造「听到就想选」的诱惑，但意大利新法是「便利捐赠」，不是「禁止超量\n生产」；禁止过量生产是后文欧洲议会的事，本项答非所问。\n借用原文：stop grocery giants from unfair trading practices that result in overproduction", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What did the European Parliament do to reduce food waste?", "options": ["A) It imposed penalties on businesses that waste food.", "B) It passed a law aiming to stop overproduction.", "C) It voted against food import from outside Europe.", "D) It prohibited the promotion of bulk food sales."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） it passed a law aiming to stop overproduction.\n【定位】Just earlier this year, the European Parliament voted in favor of legislation that would stop\ngrocery giants from unfair trading practices that result in overproduction, thus creating waste.\n就在今年早些时候，欧洲议会投票通过了一项立法，旨在阻止大型杂货商的不公平贸易行\n为，这类行为会导致过量生产、进而造成浪费。\n【信号】主体 European Parliament 后跟动作。录音点出主体 the European Parliament voted\nin favor of legislation，紧跟的 that 从句说明该立法的目的是 stop grocery giants from\nunfair trading practices that result in overproduction，主体加动作即答案句。\n【替换】voted in favor of legislation ↔ passed a law\n投票赞成立法 = 通过法律\nthat result in overproduction ↔ aiming to stop overproduction\n阻止导致过量生产的行为=阻止过量生产\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n「对浪费食物的商家处以罚款」确有其事，但出自后文 imposed harsh penalties on\nbusinesses，主语是法国政府，不是欧洲议会，把法国的做法安到欧洲议会头上，属张冠李\n戴。\n借用原文：imposed harsh penalties on businesses that fail to comply with the regulations\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项含 food import from outside Europe，借用录音里反复出现的 Europe 制造熟悉感，\n但录音从未说欧洲议会投票反对食品进口，进口一事纯属编造。\n借用原文：the European Parliament voted in favor of legislation\nD) 未提及\n选项说禁止散装食品促销（bulk food sales），录音里欧洲议会一段只谈遏制导致过量生产\n的不公平贸易，从未提「散装促销」，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What has the French government done recently?", "options": ["A) It has warned its people against possible food shortages.", "B) It has warned its people against possible food shortages.", "C) It has started a nationwide campaign against food waste.", "D) It has banned supermarkets from dumping edible foods."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） it has banned supermarkets from dumping edible foods.\n【定位】In France, the government has banned supermarkets from throwing away edible foods and\nimposed harsh penalties on businesses that fail to comply with the regulations.\n在法国，政府已禁止超市丢弃可食用的食物，并对不遵守规定的商家处以重罚。\n【信号】In France 主体后跟两个动作。录音以 In France, the government has banned\nsupermarkets from throwing away edible foods 直接交代法国政府的动作，主体加动作\n即答案句，开头的 In France 就是定位信号。\n【替换】has banned supermarkets from throwing away edible foods ↔\nit has banned supermarkets from dumping edible foods\nthrowing away = dumping\n【排除】A) 未提及\n选项说政府警告民众可能出现食物短缺，录音法国一段只谈禁止丢弃和罚款，从未提「警告\n短缺」，属未提及的编造。\nB) 未提及\n本项文字与 A 完全相同（warned its people against possible food shortages），同样\n在录音里无对应，属未提及，自然不是答案。\nC) 未提及\n选项说法国发起了全国性反浪费运动，录音法国段落只有「禁止丢弃 + 罚款」两个动作，从\n未提 nationwide campaign，属未提及。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What is the major cause of food waste in the United States?", "options": ["A) The confusion over food expiration labels.", "B) The surplus resulting from overproduction.", "C) Americans' habit of buying food in bulk.", "D) A lack of regulation on food consumption."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） the confusion over food expiration labels.\n【定位】One major cause of this problem is the confusion over food expiration labels, which are\ncurrently not regulated by the government.\n这个问题的一个主要原因是人们对食品保质期标签的困惑，而这些标签目前并不受政府\n监管。\n【信号】信号词 One major cause is。录音直接用 One major cause of this problem is the\nconfusion over food expiration labels 点出原因，「One major cause...is」就是因果信\n号词，其后的名词正是答案。\n【替换】the confusion over food expiration labels ↔ the confusion over food expiration labels\n原句近乎照搬\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 the surplus resulting from overproduction 借用前文欧洲议会段落的\noverproduction 一词制造熟悉感，但「过量生产带来的剩余」是描述欧洲问题的，录音说\n美国浪费的主因是标签困惑，不是过量生产，属张冠李戴的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：that result in overproduction\nC) 未提及\n选项说美国人散装购买食物的习惯，录音美国段落只谈标签困惑和无监管，从未提 buying\nfood in bulk，属未提及。\nD) 偷换概念\n录音确实说 not regulated by the government，但被监管缺失的是「保质期标签」，选项\na lack of regulation on food consumption 把「不受监管的标签」偷换成「对食品消费缺\n乏监管」，对象被悄悄改成 consumption，答非所问。\n借用原文：which are currently not regulated by the government", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What do we learn about Saks department store?", "options": ["A) It has started a week-long promotion campaign.", "B) It has just launched its annual anniversary sales.", "C) It offers regular weekend sales all the year round.", "D) It specializes in the sale of ladies' designer dresses."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） it has just launched its annual anniversary sales.\n【定位】Yes. Our anniversary sales started on Monday.\n是的。我们的周年庆特卖周一就开始了。\n【信号】店员对店情的直接确认。顾客先在 the annual anniversary sales had begun 提出周年庆\n已开始，店员用 Yes. Our anniversary sales started on Monday. 当面确认——这种\"被问\n者的直接肯定应答\"就是答案信号，确认句里的事实即店情真相。\n【替换】Our anniversary sales started on Monday ↔ it has just launched its annual anniversary sales\nstarted on Monday = just launched\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确有 we are running a promotion for complimentary tailoring，A 借 promotion\n一词制造\"听到就想选\"，但那是免费改衣的促销、且非\"为期一周\"，本题问的店情核心是周\n年庆开张，A 答非所问。\n借用原文：we are running a promotion\nC) 偷换概念\n录音说的是 anniversary sales started on Monday(一年一度、刚开始)，C 却说 regular\nweekend sales all the year round(常年都有的周末特卖)，把\"年度限时\"偷换成\"常年\"，与\n原文性质相反。\n借用原文：anniversary sales started on Monday\nD) 未提及\n对话后段虽提到顾客看中 a dress，但录音从未说这家店\"专营女士设计师裙装\"，D 把一件\n商品放大成\"经营品类\"，属无中生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What does the man say Saks department store offers?", "options": ["A) Price reductions for its frequent customers.", "B) Coupons for customers with bulk purchases.", "C) Free delivery of purchases for senior customers.", "D) Price adjustments within seven days of purchase."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） price adjustments within seven days of purchase.\n【定位】We do offer price adjustments within 7 days of purchase to ensure our customers' satisfaction.\n我们确实提供购买后 7 天内的价格调整，以确保顾客满意。\n【信号】店员用 offer 直接报政策。店员一句 We do offer price adjustments within 7 days of\npurchase 与题干 offer 同源，do 加重语气强调\"确实提供\"——这种\"说话人亲口用 offer 报\n出政策\"就是答案信号，直接锁定该句。\n【替换】price adjustments within 7 days of purchase ↔ price adjustments within seven days of purchase\n数字 7 = 拼写 seven，余几乎原句\n【排除】A) 未提及\n录音里顾客说朋友买到 a much lower price，A 借\"降价\"的印象编出 price reductions for\nits frequent customers，但原文从未提\"给老客户专门降价\"，对象与政策都是无中生有。\nB) 未提及\ncoupons for customers with bulk purchases 说的优惠券与大批量购买，录音通篇没提，\n属凭\"商场常给券\"的常识硬填。\nC) 未提及\nfree delivery of purchases for senior customers 的\"为老年顾客免费送货\"原文完全没\n有，是凭空捏造的对象+服务。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "What does the woman want the store to do to address the price difference?", "options": ["A) Mail a gift card to her.", "B) Allow her to buy on credit.", "C) Credit it to her account.", "D) Give her some coupons."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Credit it to her account.\n【定位】Crediting my account would be wonderful. Thank you.\n把差价记入我的账户就太好了。谢谢。\n【信号】顾客在二选一中的明确选定。店员在 We can credit your account directly... Otherwise,\nwe can send a gift card by mail 给出两条路，顾客用 Crediting my account would be\nwonderful 挑定第一条——这种\"被给选择者的拍板应答\"就是答案信号，选定句即答案。\n【替换】Crediting my account would be wonderful ↔ Credit it to her account.\nCrediting my account = Credit it to her account\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nMail a gift card to her. 确实被店员当备选说出 we can send a gift card by mail，但那是\n顾客没选的 Otherwise 方案；盗用这句原词诱你选\"听过的\"，实则被放弃。\n借用原文：we can send a gift card by mail\nB) 偷换概念\nAllow her to buy on credit. 借 credit 与原文 credit your account 同词，但 buy on\ncredit 是\"赊账消费\"，与\"把差价记入账户\"完全两回事，是同词分叉的偷换。\n借用原文：credit your account\nD) 未提及\nGive her some coupons. 的优惠券原文压根没提，更不是顾客处理差价选定的方式，无中\n生有。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What is the service Saks department store offers in addition to the promotion sale?", "options": ["A) Refunding for goods returned.", "B) Free installing of appliances.", "C) Prolonged goods warranty.", "D) Complimentary tailoring."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） complimentary tailoring.\n【定位】In addition to the sale, we are running a promotion for complimentary tailoring if you need it.\n除了打折之外，我们还在搞一个免费裁改的活动，如果你需要的话。\n【信号】追加信号 In addition to。店员用 In addition to the sale 这个\"追加\"信号词领出促销之外\n的内容，信号后紧跟 complimentary tailoring——题干 in addition 与录音 In addition\nto 对位，信号后那条服务即答案。\n【替换】complimentary tailoring ↔ complimentary tailoring\ncomplimentary tailoring 原词对应\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音谈的是 price adjustments(退差价)，A 的 refunding for goods returned 是\"退货退\n款\"，话题相近但性质不同，且不是\"促销之外的额外服务\"，借\"退\"的印象设陷。\n借用原文：price adjustments\nB) 未提及\nfree installing of appliances 的免费安装家电，录音通篇没提，凭\"商场附加服务\"常识硬\n填。\nC) 未提及\n录音只说改衣 completed within 5 working days(5个工作日完工)，C 的 prolonged\ngoods warranty\"延长保修\"是另一回事，借\"时间/期限\"的印象误导。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What do we know about the original Barbie dolls?", "options": ["A) They are thin, tall, and unlike real human beings.", "B) They have more than twenty different hair textures.", "C) They have twenty-four different body shapes in total.", "D) They represent people from virtually all walks of life."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） they are thin, tall, and unlike real human beings.\n【定位】They're thin, tall, long-legged … and virtually unlike any real human being.\n它们又瘦又高、腿很长……而且几乎不像任何真人。\n【信号】开篇对原版的定性描写句。第一段开头先说芭比有其独特长相，紧接 They're thin, tall,\nlong-legged … and virtually unlike any real human being 一句正面定性原版外形，是\n承载答案的描写句；这种'话题对象+一串形容词'的句子就是细节题的落点。\n【替换】thin, tall ↔ thin, tall\n原词照搬，描述一致\nvirtually unlike any real human being ↔ unlike real human beings\n近义改写，单复数微调\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音里 22 eye colours and 24 hair styles 确实念到了发型与数字，B 借此设陷；但这些是\n第二段给'新款'添加的，并非问的'原版(original)'，而且 more than twenty different hair\ntextures 把数字与对象都套错了，属答非所问。\n借用原文：22 eye colours and 24 hair styles\nC) 原词陷阱\ntwenty-four different body shapes 里的 twenty-four 来自录音 24 hair styles，但 24 修\n饰的是发型(hair styles)不是身材(body shapes)，且仍是新款而非原版，数字与对象双重张\n冠李戴。\n借用原文：24 hair styles\nD) 与原文相反\nrepresent people from virtually all walks of life 说原版能代表各阶层的人，但录音明确\n说原版 virtually unlike any real human being(不像任何真人)，方向正好相\n反；'represent real people'是后文公司对新款的愿望，被挪来形容原版。\n借用原文：virtually unlike any real human being", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "Why do some people feel unsatisfied with the new Barbie dolls?", "options": ["A) They do not reflect young girls' aspirations.", "B) They are not sold together with the original.", "C) Their flat feet do not appeal to adolescents.", "D) Their body shapes have not changed much."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） their body shapes have not changed much.\n【定位】Although many people say the new Barbies are a step in the right direction, some people say\nthey don't go far enough. They say that the new body shapes could be even more different\nfrom the original, tall, thin Barbies.\n尽管很多人说新款芭比是朝正确方向迈出的一步，但有些人说它做得还不够。他们说新的身\n材本可以与原版又高又瘦的芭比差别更大。\n【信号】让步Although后的批评+They say补因。Although many people say… some people\nsay they don't go far enough 用让步连词 Although 把正面评价抛在前、把批评 don't\ngo far enough 落在后，转折后才是真意；紧接 the new body shapes could be even\nmore different 用 They say 补出不满的具体原因，正是答案句。\n【替换】the new body shapes could be even more different from the original ↔\ntheir body shapes have not changed much\n反话正说，等值转换\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\ndo not reflect young girls' aspirations 里的女孩、愿望让人联想录音 girls everywhere\n… infinitely more ways to spark their imagination，但那是第四段公司的正面愿景，且录\n音从没说新款'不反映女孩愿望'，属盗用原词、答非所问。\n借用原文：girls everywhere … infinitely more ways to spark their imagination\nB) 与原文相反\nnot sold together with the original 说新款不与原版一起卖，但录音第三段说 the\noriginal, tall and thin Barbies will continue to be sold(原版会继续卖)，并无'不一起卖'之\n说，且这也不是不满的原因，方向与事实都不符。\n借用原文：the original, tall and thin Barbies will continue to be sold\nC) 原词陷阱\nflat feet do not appeal to adolescents 借用录音 gave Barbie a flat foot 的 flat foot 设\n陷，但录音里平脚是作为'改进'正面提及的，从未说它'不吸引青少年'，整句是无中生有的捏\n造。\n借用原文：gave Barbie a flat foot", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "Where will the new Barbie dolls be sold first?", "options": ["A) In toy stores", "B) In shopping malls.", "C) On the internet", "D) At Barbie shops"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） on the internet\n【定位】The toys aren't in stores yet but they will be sold online at the Barbie website, starting this\nweek, for $9.99.\n这些玩具还没在(实体)店里上架，但本周起将在芭比官网线上发售，售价 9.99 美元。\n【信号】but对比+yet表'还没/率先'。The toys aren't in stores yet but they will be sold online\n用 aren't… yet(还没在店) 与 but… online(但将在线上) 形成对比，but 之后的 online 才是\n答案；yet 与 starting this week 共同对应题干的 first(最先在线上卖)。\n【替换】sold online at the Barbie website ↔ on the internet\nonline=on the internet，上位概括\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\nin toy stores 借录音 aren't in stores yet 的 stores 设陷，但原文恰恰说'还没在店\n里'(aren't… yet)，实体店正是被否定、被排除的地点，听到 stores 就选反而中招。\n借用原文：The toys aren't in stores yet\nB) 未提及\nin shopping malls 的购物中心在整段录音里根本没出现，属凭'卖东西就该在商场'的常识捏\n造，是无中生有。\nD) 原词陷阱\nat Barbie shops 抓住录音 at the Barbie website 的 Barbie 一词，却把 website(官网/线\n上) 偷换成 shops(实体专卖店)，正是听清品牌名没听清是线上还是线下就会中的陷阱。\n借用原文：at the Barbie website", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What happened in Germany around the year 1450?", "options": ["A) Moveable metal type began to be used in printing", "B) Chinese printing technology was first introduced", "C) The earliest known book was published", "D) Metal type was imported from Korea"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） moveable metal type began to be used in printing\n【定位】but it was in Germany around the year 1450 that a printing press using movable metal type\nwas invented.\n但正是在德国、约1450年，一种使用活动金属活字的印刷机被发明出来。\n【信号】转折 but + 强调句 it was…that 双重锁定。这句以 but it was in Germany around the\nyear 1450 that 开头，转折词 but 把前面提到的中国、韩国都甩开，强调句式 it was…that\n又把 Germany、1450 重重圈出，转折后加强调正是答案信号，德国此时发生的事就在\nthat 之后。\n【替换】a printing press using movable metal type was invented ↔\nmoveable metal type began to be used in printing\n发明使用活字印刷机 = 活字开始用于印刷\n【排除】B) 张冠李戴\n录音里中国相关的是 The earliest printed book we know today appeared in China in\nthe year 868，那是868年的中国、且讲的是最早印本书，与本题问的德国1450年完全错\n位，选项把中国印刷术引入安到这个时间点上属张冠李戴。\n借用原文：The earliest printed book we know today appeared in China in the year 868\nC) 原词陷阱\nThe earliest known book was published 借用了录音 The earliest printed book we\nknow today 的原词，但那件事发生在868年的中国，不是德国1450年，听到 earliest\nbook 就选正中陷阱。\n借用原文：The earliest printed book we know today appeared in China in the year 868\nD) 偷换概念\nmetal type was imported from Korea 拼接了录音里的 metal type was in use in Korea\nat the beginning of the fifteenth century，但录音说的是韩国在用金属活字，从没说德国\n从韩国进口，且那是15世纪初的韩国，不是1450年的德国，既偷换概念又时间地点错位。\n借用原文：metal type was in use in Korea at the beginning of the fifteenth century", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "What does the speaker say about the printer, Plantin of Antwerp?", "options": ["A) It had more than a hundred printing presses.", "B) It was the biggest printer in the 16th century.", "C) It helped the German people become literate.", "D) It produced some 20 million volumes in total"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） it was the biggest printer in the 16th century.\n【定位】The biggest sixteenth-century printer, Plantin of Antwerp, had twenty-four printing presses\nand employed more than a hundred workers.\n16世纪最大的印刷商，安特卫普的Plantin，拥有24台印刷机，雇佣了一百多名工人。\n【信号】最高级 the biggest 直接定性。这句开头 The biggest sixteenth-century printer, Plantin\nof Antwerp 用最高级 the biggest 直接给这家印刷商定性，最高级在听力里常是答案信\n号，对它的地位评价就藏在这个最高级里。\n【替换】The biggest sixteenth-century printer ↔ it was the biggest printer in the 16th century\n几乎原词对应的最高级表述\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音说 had twenty-four printing presses and employed more than a hundred\nworkers，是24台印刷机、一百多名工人，A项 it had more than a hundred printing\npresses 把 a hundred 这个数字从工人挪到了印刷机上，数字是真的，单位被偷换了。\n借用原文：had twenty-four printing presses and employed more than a hundred workers\nC) 未提及\n录音另一处说 Only a small fraction of the population was literate，C项 it helped the\nGerman people become literate 借用了 literate 一词，还顺手拉来话题里的德国，但录\n音从没说这家印刷商帮德国人识了字，属强加因果、无中生有。\n借用原文：Only a small fraction of the population was literate\nD) 张冠李戴\n录音 By 1500 some twenty million volumes had already been printed 说的是到1500年\n全行业总共印了约两千万册，D项 it produced some 20 million volumes in total 把这个\n全行业数字安到 Plantin 这一家头上，数字真但主语错了。\n借用原文：By 1500 some twenty million volumes had already been printed", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What was the immediate effect of printing?", "options": ["A) It pushed handwritten books out of circulation.", "B) It boosted the circulation of popular works.", "C) It made writing a very profitable career.", "D) It provided readers with more choices."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） it boosted the circulation of popular works.\n【定位】The immediate effect of printing was to increase the circulation of works that were already\npopular in a handwritten form, while less popular works went out of circulation.\n印刷术的直接影响是增加了那些以手稿形式本就受欢迎的作品的流通，而不太受欢迎的作品\n则退出了流通。\n【信号】段首主题句 + 题干同词 immediate effect。本段第一句即出现题干同款词 The\nimmediate effect of printing was to，段首主题句直接对应题干，是最强的答案信号，影\n响的具体内容紧跟其后。\n【替换】to increase the circulation of works that were already popular ↔\nit boosted the circulation of popular works\nincrease circulation of popular = boost circulation of popular works\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音说的是 less popular works went out of circulation，退出流通的是不太受欢迎的作\n品；A项 it pushed handwritten books out of circulation 把 out of circulation 这个原词\n搬来，却换成把手写书全挤出流通，主语被换、范围被夸大成绝对。\n借用原文：less popular works went out of circulation\nC) 偷换概念\n录音说 cover the costs of production and make a profit，赚钱的是出版商；C项 it\nmade writing a very profitable career 借用 profit 一词，却把它扯到写作成为赚钱职业\n上，录音从没说写作变成了盈利行当，属偷换概念。\n借用原文：cover the costs of production and make a profit\nD) 与原文相反\n录音段尾明确说 it also reduced choice，印刷术也减少了选择；D项 it provided readers\nwith more choices 说给读者更多选择，方向与原文完全相反，是典型的正话反说。\n借用原文：it also reduced choice", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What was the great cultural impact of printing?", "options": ["A) It accelerated the extinction of the Latin language.", "B) It standardized the publication of grammar books.", "C) Turned translation into a welcome profession.", "D) It promoted the growth of national languages"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） it promoted the growth of national languages\n【定位】The great cultural impact of printing was that it facilitated the growth of national languages.\n印刷术重大的文化影响在于它推动了民族语言的发展。\n【信号】段首主题句 + 题干同词 great cultural impact。本段第一句就是 The great cultural\nimpact of printing was that it facilitated the growth of national languages，与题干\ngreat cultural impact 完全撞词，段首主题句直接给出结论，是最强答案信号，后面\nLatin、grammar books 都是对它的展开论证。\n【替换】it facilitated the growth of national languages ↔ it promoted the growth of national languages\nfacilitated = promoted 的同义替换\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n录音只说 the market for Latin was limited，拉丁语市场有限；A项 it accelerated the\nextinction of the Latin language 借用 Latin 一词，却夸张成加速拉丁语灭绝，市场有限\n不等于灭绝，属过度推断。\n借用原文：the market for Latin was limited\nB) 原词陷阱\n录音 producing dictionaries and grammar books 里 grammar books 只是印刷术固定\n语言的手段之一，B项 standardized the publication of grammar books 把这个细节词搬\n来当作重大影响，是听到原词就想选的陷阱，且原文是用语法书去规范语言，不是规范语法\n书的出版。\n借用原文：producing dictionaries and grammar books\nC) 未提及\n录音说 the book trade soon produced translations into the national languages，书业\n产出了译本；C项 turned translation into a welcome profession 借用 translation 一\n词，却说让翻译成了受欢迎的职业，录音从没说翻译变成一种职业，属无中生有。\n借用原文：the book trade soon produced translations into the national languages", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the speaker say about many college graduates?", "options": ["A) It promoted the growth of national languages", "B) They spend an average of one year finding a job.", "C) They become stuck in the same job for decades.", "D) They choose a job without thinking it through."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They choose a job without thinking it through.\n【定位】Many people graduate from school or college not knowing what to do with their lives, and get\na job without really thinking about it.\n很多人从中学或大学毕业时并不知道自己想做什么，于是没怎么认真考虑就随便找了份\n工作。\n【信号】群体词 graduate 直接点名 + 行为描述。答案句以 Many people graduate from school\nor college 点名了题干里的\"college graduates\"群体，紧接着 get a job without really\nthinking about it 描述他们的行为，群体词一出现就锁定这句，这是篇章题靠\"对象词定\n位\"的典型信号。\n【替换】get a job without really thinking about it ↔ They choose a job without thinking it through.\nget a job≈choose a job，without thinking≈without thinking it through\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\nThey spend an average of one year finding a job. 盗用了录音里 Your working life\nlasts an average of forty years 的 average 和数字感，但原文的\"average\"修饰的是\"工作\n生涯长达四十年\"，根本不是\"花一年找工作\"，是把数字嫁接到错误对象上。\n借用原文：Your working life lasts an average of forty years\nC) 原词陷阱\nThey become stuck in the same job for decades. 盗用了录音 stuck in a job they hate\n的\"stuck\"，但原文说的是\"困在一份讨厌的工作里\"，并没说\"同一份工作几十年(for\ndecades)\"，凭空加了时间夸大，且原文这是\"others\"(部分人)的情形、不是讲\"many\ncollege graduates\"的整体说法。\n借用原文：others often find themselves stuck in a job they hate\nA) 未提及\nIt promoted the growth of national languages 既与\"选职业\"主题无关，录音里也根本没\n有 national languages 的任何内容，是和全篇脱节的无关干扰。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What does the Australian website suggest you do first to find a suitable job?", "options": ["A) See if there will be chances for promotion.", "B) Find out what job choices are available.", "C) Watch a film about ways of job hunting.", "D) Decide which job is most attractive to you."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） find out what job choices are available.\n【定位】Before you see a movie, you find out what films are showing. The site suggests you should\ndo the same with your career—find out what jobs are available and what your options are.\n看电影前，你会先查有哪些电影在上映。该网站建议你对职业也照此办理——弄清有哪些工\n作可选、你都有哪些选项。\n【信号】次序词 Before + 类比 do the same 引出第一步。Before you see a movie, you find out\nwhat films are showing 用 Before 标出\"第一步\"，再用 do the same with your career\n把看电影的第一步类比到选职业，紧接着 find out what jobs are available and what\nyour options are 就是落到职业上的第一步动作，次序词加类比是这句成为答案句的信号。\n【替换】find out what jobs are available and what your options are ↔\nfind out what job choices are available.\nwhat jobs are available + your options=what job choices are available\n【排除】C) 原词陷阱\nwatch a film about ways of job hunting. 盗用了录音反复出现的 Before you see a\nmovie 里的 movie/film，但录音是拿\"看电影\"打比方，从没建议你真去看一部关于找工作\n的电影，把比喻误当字面动作。\n借用原文：Before you see a movie, you find out what films are showing\nD) 答非所问\ndecide which job is most attractive to you. 对应录音 decide which movie you like\nbest/decide which job will suit your personality，但那是类比里的\"第二步\"(决定喜欢哪\n个)，题干问的是 first，答非所问。\n借用原文：Next, decide which movie you like best\nA) 未提及\nsee if there will be chances for promotion. 讲\"晋升机会\"，录音讲第一步时只说\"弄清有\n哪些工作\"，全篇没有 promotion 的对应内容，属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What should you think about when you look for the right job according to the Australian website?", "options": ["A) The qualifications you have.", "B) The pay you are going to get.", "C) The culture of your target company.", "D) The work environment you will be in."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） the qualifications you have.\n【定位】Some jobs require you to have certain life experiences: Have you traveled overseas? Do you\nhave any extra certificates besides your degree, such as a first aid license, for example?\n有些工作要求你具备某些人生经历：你出过国吗？除了学位，你还有别的证书吗，比如急救\n执照？\n【信号】require you to have + 列举证书学历，对应\"资历\"。Some jobs require you to have\ncertain life experiences 以\"有些工作要求你具备……\"领起，随后列举 extra certificates\nbesides your degree, such as a first aid license，证书、学位这些正是\"资历\"，是该句承\n载答案的信号。\n【替换】any extra certificates besides your degree, such as a first aid license ↔\nthe qualifications you have.\ncertificates/degree/license 是 qualifications 的下位例子\n【排除】C) 偷换概念\nthe culture of your target company. 看似呼应录音谈的\"personality / suit\"，但录音\ndecide which job will suit your personality 说的是工作要合\"你的个性\"，不是\"目标公司\n的文化\"，把\"个人个性\"偷换成\"公司文化\"。\n借用原文：decide which job will suit your personality\nD) 偷换概念\nthe work environment you will be in. 借了录音 If you like working alone、physical\nstate 等说法的影子，但录音讲的是\"你的体能状态、是否喜欢独自工作\"这类自身条件，不\n是客观的\"工作环境\"，属概念偏移。\n借用原文：Your physical state and build can also affect which jobs you can do\nB) 未提及\nthe pay you are going to get. 是人人觉得择业\"当然要看\"的因素，但该网站谈的是 life\nexperiences、certificates、physical state、personality，从头到尾没提 pay/salary，纯\n属凭常识硬填。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the speaker say about Kwanzaa?", "options": ["A) It is as important as Christmas for African-Americans.", "B) It is a cultural festival founded for African-Americans.", "C) It is an ancient festival celebrated by African-Americans.", "D) It is a religious festival celebrated by African-Americans."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It is a cultural festival founded for African-Americans.\n【定位】Kwanzaa is a cultural festival during which African-Americans celebrate and reflect upon\ntheir rich heritage as the products of two worlds.\nKwanzaa 是一个文化节日，非裔美国人在节日期间庆祝并反思他们作为两个世界产物的丰\n厚传统。\n【信号】开篇下定义句 is a cultural festival。录音开门见山一句 Kwanzaa is a cultural festival\nduring which African-Americans celebrate and reflect upon their rich heritage，这种\nX is a … 的定义句就是答案信号，节日的定性标签 cultural festival 直接给出。\n【替换】a cultural festival ↔ a cultural festival\n定性标签原义照搬，cultural 对 cultural\nduring which African-Americans celebrate ↔ for African-Americans\n为谁而设，对象一致\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt is as important as Christmas for African-Americans. 拿它和圣诞节比重要性，录音从\n头到尾没有任何 与圣诞节同等重要 的比较，纯属臆造。\nC) 与原文相反\nan ancient festival 借用了录音真有的 festival，但把它说成 古老的；录音明说这是经研究\n后新设立的节日（the new holiday），与 ancient 正相反。\n借用原文：the new holiday\nD) 与原文相反\na religious festival 同样借 festival 设饵，却把性质改成宗教；录音明确说它是 a cultural\nfestival，文化与宗教正相反。\n借用原文：a cultural festival", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "For what purpose did Dr. Karenga create the special holiday?", "options": ["A) To urge African-Americans to do more for society.", "B) To call on African-Americans to worship their gods.", "C) To help African-Americans to realize their goals.", "D) To remind African-Americans of their sufferings."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （目的题） to help African-Americans to realize their goals.\n【定位】Kwanzaa is based on seven principles which are unity, self-determination, collective work\nand responsibility, cooperative economics, purpose, creativity and faith. One principle is\nhighlighted each day of the holiday.\nKwanzaa 建立在七项原则之上，分别是团结、自决、集体劳动与责任、合作经济、目标、\n创造力和信仰。节日期间每天突出其中一项原则。\n【信号】节日围绕七项原则展开，指向其引导作用。录音点出这个节日 is based on seven\nprinciples，并逐日强调一项原则（One principle is highlighted each day of the\nholiday.），表明设立它是为了用这些积极原则引导非裔美国人，落点对应帮助其实现\n目标。\n【替换】seven principles which are unity, self-determination, collective work and responsibility, cooperative\neconomics, purpose, creativity and faith\n↔ to realize their goals\n目标 purpose 与 goals 同义\nOne principle is highlighted each day of the holiday. ↔ to help African-Americans\n逐日强调原则即对人的引导帮助\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nto urge African-Americans to do more for society. 把目的说成督促为社会多做事，录音\n只讲个人围绕原则反思，并无 为社会多做贡献 的诉求，是无据的引申。\nB) 偷换概念\nto call on African-Americans to worship their gods. 借用了录音七原则里的 faith（信\n仰），却把它偷换成 敬拜神明；此处的 faith 是世俗原则之一，并非宗教崇拜，录音也强调\n这是文化节而非宗教节。\n借用原文：faith\nD) 答非所问\nto remind African-Americans of their sufferings. 把目的定在回顾苦难上，录音讲创立宗\n旨时落在积极原则与传统传承，并无 让人记住苦难 这一目的（红蜡烛象征 struggles 是象\n征意义，非创立目的）。\n借用原文：the red is for their struggles", "points": ["听力·讲座", "目的题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What does the word \"Kwanzaa\" mean?", "options": ["A) Faith in self-determination.", "B) The first fruits of the harvest.", "C) Unity and cooperative economics.", "D) Creative work and achievement."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） the first fruits of the harvest.\n【定位】Kwanzaa is a Kiswahili word meaning \"the first fruits of the harvest.\"\nKwanzaa 是一个斯瓦希里语词，意思是 收获的初熟果实。\n【信号】释义句 a word meaning …。录音明用 a Kiswahili word meaning \"the first fruits of\nthe harvest.\"，meaning 一词就是答案信号，后面引号里的内容就是该词的字面含义。\n【替换】the first fruits of the harvest ↔ the first fruits of the harvest.\n释义内容原义对应\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nfaith in self-determination. 拼用了录音七原则中的 self-determination 和 faith，但那是\n节日的原则、不是 Kwanzaa 这个词的意思，答非所问。\n借用原文：self-determination\nC) 原词陷阱\nunity and cooperative economics. 取自录音七原则里的 unity 和 cooperative\neconomics，同样是原则而非词义，与本题问的 the word mean 不对应。\n借用原文：cooperative economics\nD) 原词陷阱\ncreative work and achievement. 化用了录音七原则里的 creativity，把原则改头换面当词\n义；Kwanzaa 的字面意思是收获初果，与 创造性工作 无关。\n借用原文：creativity", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What do people do while each candle is being lit at the Kwanzaa celebration?", "options": ["A) They recite a principle.", "B) They take a solemn oath.", "C) They drink wine from the unity cup.", "D) They call out their ancestors' names."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） they recite a principle.\n【定位】While the candle is being lit, a principle is recited; then each person present takes a turn to\nspeak about the importance that principle has to himself or herself.\n在点蜡烛的同时，会诵读一项原则；然后在场每个人轮流讲述这项原则对自己的意义。\n【信号】题干 while 句与原文 While 句严丝合缝。录音 While the candle is being lit, a principle\nis recited 的 While… 句与题干 while each candle is being lit 完全对应，紧跟的 a\nprinciple is recited 就是同时发生的动作，正是答案信号。\n【替换】a principle is recited ↔ they recite a principle.\n被动转主动，动作与宾语一致\n【排除】B) 未提及\nthey take a solemn oath. 说人们郑重宣誓，录音通篇没有任何 宣誓 的环节，纯属添加。\nC) 张冠李戴\nthey drink wine from the unity cup. 借用了录音真有的 the unity cup 和饮用动作，但那\n发生在后面的缅怀环节（That person then drinks from the cup），并非 点蜡烛的同时；\n时点错配。\n借用原文：That person then drinks from the cup\nD) 张冠李戴\nthey call out their ancestors' names. 取自录音的 names of African-American leaders\nand heroes are called out，但那在缅怀逝者的仪式段，发生在点蜡烛之后，不是 while …\nis being lit 这一时点。\n借用原文：names of African-American leaders and heroes are called out", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What has research concluded about the Mediterranean diet?", "options": ["A) It is one of the world's most healthy diets.", "B) It contains large amounts of dairy products.", "C) It began to impact the world in recent years.", "D) It consists mainly of various kinds of seafood."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It is one of the world's most healthy diets.\n【定位】Several noted nutritionists and research projects have concluded that this diet is one of the\nmost healthful in the world in terms of preventing such illnesses as heart disease and cancer,\nand increasing life expectancy.\n几位知名营养学家和多项研究项目得出结论：就预防心脏病、癌症等疾病和延长寿命而言，\n这种饮食是世界上最健康的饮食之一。\n【信号】下结论动词concluded。答案信号是结论动词 have concluded that，它直接对应题干的\nconcluded，后面那句 this diet is one of the most healthful in the world 就是研究下的\n结论，是本题落点。\n【替换】this diet is one of the most healthful in the world ↔ It is one of the world's most healthy diets.\nhealthful=healthy同义改写\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音确实念到 dairy products，但说的是 Mediterranean cooking uses smaller amounts\nof saturated fat，而饱和脂肪主要来自肉和乳制品，等于乳制品是少用的，选项 contains\nlarge amounts of dairy products 把少说成大量，盗用原词却说反了。\n借用原文：Mediterranean cooking uses smaller amounts of saturated fat\nC) 与原文相反\n录音说国际关注始于 back in the late 1950s，而这饮食习惯 developed over thousands\nof years，根本不是近年才影响世界，选项 began to impact the world in recent years 与\n原文相反。\n借用原文：began back in the late 1950s\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音只说 the Mediterranean Sea has historically yielded abundant quantities of\nfish，鱼只是物产之一，与水果蔬菜、面包、橄榄油并列，选项 consists mainly of various\nkinds of seafood 用 mainly 把鱼夸成主体，是以偏概全。\n借用原文：the Mediterranean Sea has historically yielded abundant quantities of fish", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What do we learn about the Seven Countries Study?", "options": ["A) It involved 13,000 researchers from Asia, Europe and America.", "B) It was conducted in seven mid-eastern countries in the 1950s.", "C) It is regarded as one of the greatest researches of its kind.", "D) It has drawn the attention of medical doctors the world over."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It is regarded as one of the greatest researches of its kind.\n【定位】Entitled the Seven Countries Study, it is considered one of the greatest studies of its kind\never performed.\n这项研究名为《七国研究》，被认为是同类研究中有史以来最伟大的研究之一。\n【信号】拔高评价句considered。答案信号是评价动词 it is considered one of the greatest\nstudies of its kind ever performed，这种 is considered one of the greatest 的拔高句\n正是对研究地位下的定性，是本题落点。\n【替换】it is considered one of the greatest studies of its kind ever performed ↔\nIt is regarded as one of the greatest researches of its kind.\nconsidered=regarded、study=research\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音说数据来自 nearly 13,000 men in Greece, Italy…，13,000 是被调查的男性、来自七\n个国家，选项 13,000 researchers from Asia, Europe and America 把被研究者偷换成研\n究者、把国别也改了，是张冠李戴。\n借用原文：nearly 13,000 men in Greece, Italy, Croatia, Serbia, Japan, Finland, the Netherlands,\nand the United States\nB)\n录音说研究始于 back in the late 1950s，1950s 这个时间没错，但参与国是希腊、意大\n利、日本、芬兰等遍布多洲的国家，并非 seven mid-eastern countries，选项把国别说成\n七个中东国家，与原文相反。\n借用原文：in Greece, Italy, Croatia, Serbia, Japan, Finland, the Netherlands, and the United States\nD) 未提及\n录音只说 International interest in the therapeutic qualities of the Mediterranean diet\nbegan，讲的是国际兴趣的开端，并没说这项研究引起了全世界医生的注意，选项 drawn\nthe attention of medical doctors the world over 属未提及。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "What do we learn about the Mediterranean people from the Seven Countries Study?", "options": ["A) They care much about their health.", "B) They eat foods with little fat.", "C) They use little oil in cooking.", "D) They have lower mortality rates."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） they have lower mortality rates.\n【定位】The Mediterranean groups had lower mortality rates in all age brackets and from all causes,\nparticularly from heart disease.\n地中海人群在所有年龄段、由所有死因导致的死亡率都更低，尤其是心脏病导致的死亡率。\n【信号】结论后的研究发现句。答案信号是紧跟结论 It concluded that the Mediterranean people\nin the study enjoyed some significant health advantages 之后的具体发现 The\nMediterranean groups had lower mortality rates，它把抽象的健康优势落实为可测的死\n亡率数据，是本题落点。\n【替换】The Mediterranean groups had lower mortality rates ↔ they have lower mortality rates.\ngroups=they近乎原词\n【排除】A) 过度推断\n录音讲的是研究测出的客观结果 significant health advantages 和死亡率，从没说这群人\n主观上 care much about their health，这是把客观数据推成主观态度，过度推断。\nB) 与原文相反\n录音明确说 the Mediterranean diet is as high or higher in fat than other diets，脂肪\n并不低，选项 eat foods with little fat 与原文相反，盗用了 fat 一词却说反了高低。\n借用原文：the Mediterranean diet is as high or higher in fat than other diets\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音说脂肪 mostly in the form of olive oil，强调的是用橄榄油这种不饱和脂肪、用量并\n不小，选项 use little oil in cooking 既盗用 oil 又说成少用油，与原文相反，且这题问的是\n研究发现的结果而非烹饪习惯，答非所问。\n借用原文：higher amounts of unsaturated fat, mostly in the form of olive oil", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) dependent", "B) Embarking", "C) emergency", "D) feeding", "E) grazes", "F) halted", "G) handful", "H) multitude", "I) originated", "J) reduction", "K) reproduces", "L) security", "M) terror", "N) unchecked", "O) untouch"], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C) emergency\n名词\n【词性槽】介词 of 后接名词\n【依据】固定搭配 state of emergency\nstate of emergency 是新闻里几乎不变的固定表达『紧急状态』，与上文 The insect,\nTutaabsoluta, has destroyed 80% of farms in Kaduna, Nigeria's largest tomato\nproducing state 害虫造成 80% 农田被毁的严重程度也吻合——政府宣布紧急状态。\n【竞争词】G) handful 搭配不成立\nhandful 通常用 a handful of＝一把，不会说 state of handful，搭配上根本不通。\nH) multitude 搭配不成立\nmultitude 也常以 a multitude of 出现，state of multitude 没有这种说法。\nJ) reduction 义不对题\nreduction 意为『减少』，state of reduction 既非固定搭配，与 80% 农田被毁后政府的反应\n（应是宣布紧急/告急）也不匹配。\nL) security 义反\nstate of security 意为『安全状态』，与害虫摧毁 80% 农田的灾情完全相反。且 L 在第 32 空\n有更明确归宿 food security。\nM) terror 色彩过重\nstate of terror 多指『恐怖状态』（如战乱/恐袭），程度与色彩都过头，新闻里描述农业灾情不\n会用这种政治化表达。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 of 后接名词"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) dependent", "B) Embarking", "C) emergency", "D) feeding", "E) grazes", "F) halted", "G) handful", "H) multitude", "I) originated", "J) reduction", "K) reproduces", "L) security", "M) terror", "N) unchecked", "O) untouch"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) feeding\n动词\n【词性槽】介词 by + ___ 与并列 -ing 形式\n【依据】feed on 以…为食\nfeed on 是描述昆虫/动物『以…为食』的固定搭配，与 on fruits 直接咬合；上下文\ndevastates crops by __(27)__ on fruits and digging into and moving through stalks.\n讲虫子如何破坏作物，吃果实正是破坏方式之一。\n【竞争词】B) Embarking 搭配错位\nembark on 意为『着手、开始（某事/旅程）』，主语应是人或机构开始项目，与 fruits 搭配既\n不合习惯也与『破坏作物』的语义无关。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·介词 by + ___ 与并列 -ing"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) dependent", "B) Embarking", "C) emergency", "D) feeding", "E) grazes", "F) halted", "G) handful", "H) multitude", "I) originated", "J) reduction", "K) reproduces", "L) security", "M) terror", "N) unchecked", "O) untouch"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) reproduces\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 It + ___ + 副词 → 谓语第三人称单数\n【依据】繁殖呼应 12 generations\nreproduces 意为『繁殖』，紧接的 breeding up to 12 generations per year if\nconditions are favorable. 用 breeding（繁殖）和 generations（代）做同义复现，几乎\n是『把答案写在了下半句』。\n【竞争词】E) grazes 义不对题\ngrazes 指『（食草动物）吃草』或『擦伤』，主语是害虫且后句讲『每年繁殖 12 代』，与吃草\n毫无关系。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 E) —— 看半句不看全句。只看 It ___ incredibly quickly 时，\ngrazes 也读得通；忽略下半句 breeding up to 12 generations per year 的同义复现就会\n误选。防坑：选词填空读到逗号、读到 and 都必须读到句尾再下手。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·主语 It + ___ + 副词 → 谓"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) dependent", "B) Embarking", "C) emergency", "D) feeding", "E) grazes", "F) halted", "G) handful", "H) multitude", "I) originated", "J) reduction", "K) reproduces", "L) security", "M) terror", "N) unchecked", "O) untouch"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) originated\n动词\n【词性槽】助动词 to have + 过去分词\n【依据】originate in 地点 = 起源于\noriginate in + 地点意为『起源于某地』，与下句 and later spread to Europe before\ncrossing over to sub-Saharan Africa. 后来传到欧洲、再传到撒哈拉以南非洲构成『起源\n→传播』的标准链条。\n【竞争词】F) halted 义反\nhalted in South America 意为『在南美被中止』，与下文紧接的『传到欧洲再到非洲』完全反\n向。且 F 在 31 空更贴切。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·助动词 to have + 过去分词"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) dependent", "B) Embarking", "C) emergency", "D) feeding", "E) grazes", "F) halted", "G) handful", "H) multitude", "I) originated", "J) reduction", "K) reproduces", "L) security", "M) terror", "N) unchecked", "O) untouch"], "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G) handful\n名词\n【词性槽】a ___ of 名词复数\n【依据】量与价格逻辑相称\na handful of tomatoes『一把番茄』的零售价从 0.5 升到 2.5 美元，价格涨幅描述合理；\n指零售层面消费者买一小把的常见单位。\n【竞争词】H) multitude 量级错位\na multitude of 意为『大量的、众多的』，强调数目庞大，与零售层面『从 0.5 涨到 2.5 美元』\n的小份零售价对不上：大量番茄不可能只卖 2.5 美元。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 H) —— a ___ of 双量词陷阱。G) handful 与 H) multitude 都符合\na ___ of N 结构，词性、搭配、形态全过；唯一靠『代入后量级与 from $0.50 to $2.50 价\n位是否相称』来判别——多数考生不做这一步常识校验。防坑：填完量词一定回头算一算量\n与数字配不配。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·a ___ of 名词复数"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) dependent", "B) Embarking", "C) emergency", "D) feeding", "E) grazes", "F) halted", "G) handful", "H) multitude", "I) originated", "J) reduction", "K) reproduces", "L) security", "M) terror", "N) unchecked", "O) untouch"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) halted\n动词\n【词性槽】has + ___ + 名词宾语 → p.p.\n【依据】halt production 固定搭配\nhalt production 意为『停产、中止生产』，与 due to the shortages 因番茄短缺逻辑契\n合，也与上句 Farmers are reporting steep losses 农户损失惨重的语境一致。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·has + ___ + 名词宾语 → p"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) dependent", "B) Embarking", "C) emergency", "D) feeding", "E) grazes", "F) halted", "G) handful", "H) multitude", "I) originated", "J) reduction", "K) reproduces", "L) security", "M) terror", "N) unchecked", "O) untouch"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) security\n名词\n【词性槽】名词修饰名词，做后位中心词\n【依据】food security 高频术语\nfood security『粮食安全』是国际新闻、农业部官员发言中的固定术语，与 Audu Ogbeh,\nNigeria's minister of agriculture, has warned that the pest may create serious\nproblems for food __(32)__ 农业部长口吻完全吻合。\n【竞争词】G) handful 搭配荒谬\nfood handful 不成词，handful 通常作 a handful of。\nH) multitude 搭配荒谬\nfood multitude 不成词，且语义『食物的众多』与『害虫造成严重问题』的指向矛盾。\nJ) reduction 搭配不通\nfood reduction 不是固定术语；且 J 留给第 35 空『供应进一步减少』更贴。\nM) terror 搭配不通\nfood terror 不存在；术语层面与农业部长警告语体不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·名词修饰名词，做后位中心词"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) dependent", "B) Embarking", "C) emergency", "D) feeding", "E) grazes", "F) halted", "G) handful", "H) multitude", "I) originated", "J) reduction", "K) reproduces", "L) security", "M) terror", "N) unchecked", "O) untouch"], "answer": "N", "analysis": "【答案】N) unchecked\n形容词\n【词性槽】has gone + ___ 作系表中的表语\n【依据】go unchecked 固定表达\ngo unchecked 意为『一直未受遏制』，与 experts are investigating how to control the\npest's damage and prevent its spread 专家正在研究如何控制蔓延形成因果呼应——正因\n之前未受遏制，现在才急需研究控制。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·has gone + ___ 作系表中的"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) dependent", "B) Embarking", "C) emergency", "D) feeding", "E) grazes", "F) halted", "G) handful", "H) multitude", "I) originated", "J) reduction", "K) reproduces", "L) security", "M) terror", "N) unchecked", "O) untouch"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) dependent\n形容词\n【词性槽】be 动词 + ___ + on 结构\n【依据】be dependent on 黄金搭配\nbe dependent on 意为『依赖』，与下句 as around 75% of the local harvest goes to\nwaste thanks to a lack of proper storage facilities. 因储存设施不足、本地番茄大量浪\n费，所以需依赖进口『天衣无缝』。\n【竞争词】N) unchecked 搭配错位\nunchecked 意为『未受遏制』，不与 on 搭配；且语义与『每年花 10 亿美元进口番茄酱』毫无\n关联。N 留给第 33 空 gone unchecked。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·be 动词 + ___ + on 结构"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) dependent", "B) Embarking", "C) emergency", "D) feeding", "E) grazes", "F) halted", "G) handful", "H) multitude", "I) originated", "J) reduction", "K) reproduces", "L) security", "M) terror", "N) unchecked", "O) untouch"], "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J) reduction\n名词\n【词性槽】形容词 further + ___ in\n【依据】进一步减少呼应 setback\nreduction 意为『减少』，与 A further __(35)__ in local supplies is yet another\nunwelcome setback to the industry. 中 further（进一步）和 setback（挫折）逻辑顺承\n——本地供应『进一步减少』正是行业的又一打击。\n【竞争词】H) multitude 义反\na further multitude in supplies 等于『供应进一步众多』，与 setback（挫折/打击）方向完全\n相反，逻辑上矛盾。\nM) terror 搭配荒谬\na further terror in local supplies 不成英文表达，且 terror 程度过重，与行业供给数据语体\n不符。", "points": ["选词填空", "词性判断·形容词 further + ___ in"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Online services are so designed that the more they are used, the more profit they generate.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】用得越多、利润越多的因果设计\n【定位】Since these services rely on advertising revenue, the more frequently you use them, the more\nmoney they make.\n由于这些服务依赖广告收入，你使用得越频繁，它们赚得就越多。\n【改写】Online services are so designed that ↔\nOnline services like Facebook, Twitter and the like, are called out as masters of manipulation\n—making products so good that people can't stop using them\n“被设计成”对应原文“by design”与“master of manipulation”\nthe more they are used, the more profit they generate ↔\nthe more frequently you use them, the more money they make\n同句式同义词替换\n【辨邻】 D The Net is designed to be an interruption system 与本题“使用越多→利润越多”不\n是同一命题，缺失商业模式这一关键链条。\nThe Net is designed to be an interruption system, a machine geared to dividing attention\nF we can change how often we receive the distracting notifications 是用户侧操作\n建议，与 the more they are used, the more profit they generate 无对应。\nwe can change how often we receive the distracting notifications that trigger our urge to check", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "The author admits using technology as an escape from the task at hand.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】作者承认用科技逃避手头任务\n【定位】I use technology as an escape. When I'm doing something I'd rather not do, or when I'm\nsomeplace I'd rather not be, I use my phone to port myself elsewhere.\n我把科技当作逃避手段。当我在做一件不想做的事，或身处一个不想待的地方时，我用手机\n把自己“送”到别处。\n【改写】The author admits ↔ I have a confession\n“承认”与“坦白”同义\nusing technology as an escape from the task at hand ↔ I use technology as an escape\n“逃避手头任务”逐字落到原句\n【辨邻】 K there's still someone who deserves scrutiny—the person holding the phone 仅\n引出主题，没有 The author admits 所要求的承认表态。\nthere's still someone who deserves scrutiny—the person holding the phone", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Checking phones at dinners is now accepted as normal but not belching.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】饭桌查手机被接受 vs 打嗝不被接受\n【定位】Now imagine the same dinner, but instead of checking their phone, the person belches(打\n嗝) loudly. Everyone notices. Unless the meal takes place in a beer house, this is considered\nbad manners.\n现在想象同一场聚餐，但这次那人不是查手机，而是大声打嗝。所有人都会注意到。除非这\n顿饭是在啤酒馆，否则这被视为失礼。\n【改写】Checking phones at dinners is now accepted as normal ↔\nSomehow, we accept it and say nothing when someone offends\n“被接受、无人吭声”等于“被视为正常”\nbut not belching ↔ Unless the meal takes place in a beer house, this is considered bad manners\n“不被接受”=“被视为失礼”\n【辨邻】 H someone takes out their phone to check who knows what 与 Barely anyone\nnotices and no one says a thing 只对应陈述句前半，缺“打嗝”这另一端。\nBarely anyone notices and no one says a thing\nJ taking one's phone out at the wrong time is worse than belching 已经把“被接受/\n不被接受”推进到“谁更严重”的下一步，与本题陈述不在同一层。\ntaking one's phone out at the wrong time is worse than belching because, unlike other minor\noffenses, checking tech is contagious", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "To make proper use of technology, we should not only increase our awareness of how it is changing but also how it is impacting us.", "options": null, "answer": "O", "analysis": "【答案】O 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】既意识到科技在变也意识到科技在变我们\n【定位】To put technology in its place, we must be conscious not only of how technology is changing,\nbut also of how it is changing us.\n要把科技放到它该在的位置，我们就必须既意识到科技自己在如何变化，也意识到它在如何\n改变我们。\n【改写】To make proper use of technology ↔ To put technology in its place\n“把科技放在适当位置”=“正确使用科技”\nnot only increase our awareness of how it is changing but also how it is impacting us ↔\nwe must be conscious not only of how technology is changing, but also of how it is changing us\nawareness=conscious、impacting=changing\n【辨邻】 N that's not necessarily a problem, that's progress 与 Q39 倡导的“双重意识”不是同\n一命题。\nThe intended result of making something better is that people use it more. That's not necessarily a\nproblem, that's progress", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "Most of us find it hard to focus on our immediate tasks because of internet distractions.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（弱锚点）\n【锚点】因网络分心而难以专注于眼前任务\n【定位】There's little doubt that nearly everyone who comes in contact with the Net has difficulty\ndisconnecting. Many of us, like Schwartz, struggle to stay focused on tasks that require more\nconcentration than it takes to post a status update.\n几乎可以肯定，几乎每个接触网络的人都难以摆脱网络；我们中许多人，和 Schwartz 一\n样，难以专注于那些比发条状态更需要集中精力的任务。\n【改写】Most of us find it hard to focus on our immediate tasks ↔\nMany of us, like Schwartz, struggle to stay focused on tasks that require more concentration\nthan it takes to post a status update\n“多数人难以专注于任务”逐项同义\nbecause of internet distractions ↔\nnearly everyone who comes in contact with the Net has difficulty disconnecting\n“因网络分心”=“接触网络后难以脱身”\n【辨邻】 A Nearly everyone I know is addicted in some measure to the internet 是引用别人\n的话，且谈“成瘾”而非题干强调的“难以专注于眼前任务”。\nNearly everyone I know is addicted in some measure to the internet\nD The Net is designed to be an interruption system 谈的是机制设计，不是 Most of\nus find it hard to focus 的普遍现象。\nThe Net is designed to be an interruption system, a machine geared to dividing attention", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "when one person starts checking their phone, the others will follow suit.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】一人查手机引发他人跟随\n【定位】Once one person looks at their phone, other people feel compelled to do the same, starting a\nchain reaction.\n一旦有人去看手机，其他人就会觉得自己也不得不看，由此引发连锁反应。\n【改写】when one person starts checking their phone ↔ Once one person looks at their phone\nwhen 与 Once 同义、starts checking 与 looks at 同义\nthe others will follow suit ↔\nother people feel compelled to do the same, starting a chain reaction\n“跟风”=“被迫做同样的事”+“连锁反应”\n【辨邻】 H someone takes out their phone to check who knows what 只描写一人行为，缺\nthe others will follow suit 这一结果。\nThen, during an interval in the conversation, someone takes out their phone to check who\nknows what", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "The great majority of smartphone users don't take the trouble to adjust their settings to suit their own purposes.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】大多数智能手机用户懒得改设置\n【定位】According to Adam Marchick, CEO of mobile marketing company Kahuna, less than 15\npercent of smartphone users ever bother to adjust their notification settings—meaning the\nremaining 85 percent of us default to the app makers' every preset trigger.\n据移动营销公司 Kahuna 的 CEO Adam Marchick 所言，不到 15% 的智能手机用户会费心\n去调通知设置，这意味着剩下 85% 的人默认照搬应用开发者预设的每一个触发。\n【改写】The great majority of smartphone users ↔ the remaining 85 percent of us\n“绝大多数”=“85%”\ndon't take the trouble to adjust their settings to suit their own purposes ↔\nless than 15 percent of smartphone users ever bother to adjust their notification settings\ndon't take the trouble=don't bother\nto suit their own purposes ↔ to suit our own needs, not the needs of the app makers\n“为自己需求服务”逐字对应\n【辨邻】 E They have an incentive to keep us hooked 是公司侧动机，没有 The great\nmajority of smartphone users 的用户行为数据。\nThese products aren't habit-forming by chance; it's by design. They have an incentive to keep\nus hooked", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "The internet is regarded by some as designed to distract our attention.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】有人认为网络被设计来分散注意力\n【定位】Nicholas Carr, author of The Shallows: what the internet is doing to our brains, wrote, \"The\nNet is designed to be an interruption system, a machine geared to dividing attention.\"\n《浅薄：互联网对我们大脑的影响》一书的作者 Nicholas Carr 写道：“网络被设计成一种\n打断系统、一种专门分割注意力的机器。”\n【改写】The internet is regarded by some ↔\nNicholas Carr, author of The Shallows: what the internet is doing to our brains, wrote\n“被某些人认为”=“某专家这样写道”\nas designed to distract our attention ↔\nThe Net is designed to be an interruption system, a machine geared to dividing attention\ndesigned to 完全相同、distract=divide\n【辨邻】 E These products aren't habit-forming by chance; it's by design 的落点是 habit-\nforming，与 designed to distract our attention 不是同一命题。\nThese products aren't habit-forming by chance; it's by design", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "The author attributes his tech addiction chiefly to his habit of putting off doing what he should do right away.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】作者把自己上瘾主要归因于拖延\n【定位】Though I desperately wanted to lay blame elsewhere, I finally had to admit that my\nbad habits had less to do with new-age technology and more to do with old-fashioned\nprocrastination(拖延).\n尽管我特别想把责任推给别处，最后还是不得不承认：我那些坏习惯与其说与新科技有关，\n不如说与老派的拖延有关。\n【改写】The author attributes his tech addiction chiefly to ↔\nmy bad habits had less to do with new-age technology and more to do with\nattributes…chiefly to=more to do with（在 less…more 对比中）\nhis habit of putting off doing what he should do right away ↔\nold-fashioned procrastination(拖延)\n“拖延”=“把该做的事推后”\n【辨邻】 M our tendency to do things against our interests 是对人类普遍倾向的归因，与 The\nauthor attributes his tech addiction 的个人归因不在同一层。\nAristotle and Socrates debated nature of \"akrasia\"—our tendency to do things against our interests", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "White-collar workers check email round the clock because it is required by their employers.", "options": null, "answer": "G", "analysis": "【答案】G 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】白领因雇主要求全天候查邮件\n【定位】We check email at all hours of the day—we're obsessed. But why? Because that's\nwhat the boss wants. For almost all white-collar jobs, email is the primary tool of\ncorporate communication.\n我们一天到晚都在查邮件——简直入迷。但为什么呢？因为这是老板想要的。对几乎所有白\n领工作来说，邮件是公司沟通的主要工具。\n【改写】White-collar workers ↔ For almost all white-collar jobs\n“白领工人”=“白领工作中的人”\ncheck email round the clock ↔ We check email at all hours of the day\nround the clock=at all hours of the day\nbecause it is required by their employers ↔ that's what the boss wants\n“老板想要”=“雇主要求”\n【辨邻】 E they rely on advertising revenue 与 required by their employers 不是同一归因。\nSince these services rely on advertising revenue, the more frequently you use them, the more\nmoney they make", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn about chemist John Pemberton?", "options": ["A) He used a strangely potent ingredient in a food supplement.", "B) He created a drink containing alcohol without breaking law.", "C) He became notorious because of the coca drink he developed.", "D) He risked breaking local law to make a drink with coca leaves."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn about chemist John Pemberton 是 What do we learn about X\n句式，问的是文中关于某人的具体陈述，属于典型细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What do we learn about chemist John Pemberton 结构平铺直叙：特殊疑问句\nWhat do we learn about + 介宾 chemist John Pemberton，无陷阱。\n【定位】At the time, coca leaf extract mixed with wine was a common tonic, and Pemberton's sweet\nbrew was a way to get around local laws prohibiting the sale of alcohol. But the other half of\nthe name presents another ingredient, less infamous, perhaps, but also strangely potent: the\nkola nut.\n当时，coca 叶提取物兑酒是一种常见滋补品，而 Pemberton 那款甜饮料是绕开当地禁酒\n法的办法。但名字的另一半呈现了另一种原料——也许不那么臭名昭著，却同样奇异强劲：\nkola nut。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nanother ingredient, less infamous, perhaps, but also strangely potent: the kola nut ↔\na strangely potent ingredient\nPemberton's sweet brew ↔ a food supplement\n预判要点是\"用了 strangely potent 原料\"，a strangely potent ingredient in a food\nsupplement 把这一要点逐字搬入，且 in a food supplement 与定位句的 tonic 语境\n吻合，故锁定 A。\nB) 与原文相反\na drink containing alcohol without breaking law 谎称这款饮料\"含酒\"。原文\nPemberton's sweet brew was a way to get around local laws prohibiting the sale\nof alcohol 恰恰相反——他的甜饮是为了绕开禁酒令，本身不含酒。\nC) 张冠李戴\nHe became notorious because of the coca drink he developed 称 Pemberton 因\ncoca 饮变得 notorious。原文 another ingredient, less infamous, perhaps 中\ninfamous 是修饰 ingredient（kola nut 相对 cocaine 较不\"声名狼藉\"），并未说\nPemberton 本人臭名昭著。\nD) 偷换概念\nHe risked breaking local law to make a drink with coca leaves 说 Pemberton 冒着\n违法风险做 coca 饮。原文 a way to get around local laws 中 get around 是\"绕开/规\n避\"，并非\"冒险触犯\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What does the passage say about kola nuts?", "options": ["A) Their commercial value was first discovered by Portuguese settlers.", "B) They contain some kind of energy boost not found in any other food.", "C) Many were shipped to Europe in the late 19th century for medicinal use.", "D) They were strange to the Europeans when first imported from West Africa."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What does the passage say about kola nuts 是询问文中关于某物的客观陈述，属于\n细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What does the passage say about kola nuts 结构清楚，无难句。\n【定位】By the late 19th century, kola nuts were being shipped by the tonne to Europe and the US.\nMany made their way into medicines, intended as a kind of energy boost.\n到 19 世纪末，kola nuts 已被吨级运往欧洲和美国。许多被用于药物，作为一种能量\n补给。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nTheir commercial value was first discovered by Portuguese settlers 说葡萄牙\"定居\n者\"先发现\"商业价值\"。原文 Europeans did not know of them until the 1500s,\nwhen Portuguese ships arrived 只说葡萄牙人最先\"知道\"，while the Portuguese\ntook part in the trade, ferrying nuts down the coast 只说他们参与贸易，未说\"首先\n发现商业价值\"，更无 settlers 一词。\nB) 与原文相反\nThey contain some kind of energy boost not found in any other food 说 kola\nnuts 有种\"其他食物里没有的\"提神成分。原文 they contain caffeine that also occurs\nnaturally in tea, coffee, and chocolate 明确说咖啡因\"也存在于茶、咖啡、巧克\n力\"——恰恰是\"其他食物里也有\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nBy the late 19th century, kola nuts were being shipped by the tonne to Europe and the US ↔\nMany were shipped to Europe in the late 19th century\nMany made their way into medicines ↔ for medicinal use\n预判要点是\"19 世纪末大量运到欧美做药\"，C 的 shipped to Europe + in the late\n19th century + for medicinal use 三个要素逐一落到 P5 同一处，毫无遗漏，故锁\n定 C。\nD) 偷换概念\nThey were strange to the Europeans when first imported from West Africa 含\"首\n次从西非进口\"的时间设定。原文 by 1620, when English explorer Richard Jobson\nmade his way up the Gambia, the nuts were still peculiar to his eyes 中 still\npeculiar 是说\"到 1620 年还是稀奇\"，并未把\"稀奇\"绑定到\"首次进口\"那一刻；且原文也\n未明说 1620 年发生\"进口\"动作。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "How come kola-extract colas became popular?", "options": ["A) Cocaine had become notorious.", "B) Alcoholic drinks were prohibited.", "C) Fountains were set up to sell them.", "D) Rights were sold to bottle the soda."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】题干 How come kola-extract colas became popular 以 How come 起头，问\"为什么变\n得流行\"，是对原文已存因果关系的直接提问。\n【拆句】How come kola-extract colas became popular 中 How come = Why，后接陈述结构，\n结构简单。\n【定位】When cocaine eventually fell from grace as a beverage ingredient, kola-extract colas\nbecame popular.\n当 cocaine 作为饮料原料最终失宠时，kola 提取物可乐变得流行起来。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\ncocaine eventually fell from grace as a beverage ingredient ↔ Cocaine had become notorious\n预判要点是\"cocaine 失宠\"，A 把 fell from grace 改成 became notorious，正是同义\n事实复述；其他三项都偏离了句内 when 所标定的原因。\nB) 答非所问\nAlcoholic drinks were prohibited 取自 Pemberton's sweet brew was a way to get\naround local laws prohibiting the sale of alcohol，这是 1880 年代造可乐的背景，\n不是\"cocaine 退场后 kola-extract colas 流行\"的原因。\nC) 无中生有\nFountains were set up to sell them 取自 Coca-Cola averaged nine servings a day\nacross all the Atlanta soda fountains where it was sold。soda fountains 是销售地\n点，非 kola-extract colas 整体流行的原因；且原文也未说\"设立 fountains\"是 colas 流\n行之因。\nD) 与原文相反\nRights were sold to bottle the soda 取自 As it grew more popular, the company\nsold rights to bottle the soda——原文用 As it grew more popular 明确把\"卖装瓶\n权\"放在\"流行起来之后\"，是结果不是原因。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What is known about the taste of Coca-Cola?", "options": ["A) It was so designed as to create addiction in consumers.", "B) It still relies on traditional kola nut extract.", "C) It has become more popular among the old.", "D) It has remained virtually unchanged since its creation."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is known about the taste of Coca-Cola 询问可口可乐口味的客观事实，属于\n细节题。\n【拆句】题干 What is known about the taste of Coca-Cola 结构清楚：被动疑问句，无难点。\n【定位】It's become so iconic that attempts to change its taste in 1985—sweetening it in a move\nprojected to boost sales—proved disastrous, with widespread anger from consumers. \"Coca-\nCola Classic\" returned to store shelves just three months after the \"New Coke\" was released.\n它已成为标志性饮品，以至于 1985 年试图改变口味——加甜以期推动销量——彻底失败，\n引发广泛消费者愤怒。\"Coca-Cola Classic\" 在 \"New Coke\" 发布仅三个月后就回到了货\n架上。\n【选项】A) 过度推断\nIt was so designed as to create addiction in consumers 说口味是\"为了让消费者上\n瘾\"而设计。原文只提到 capable of sparking particular devotion in consumers\n——\"激起特别热爱\"，且这是早期 cocaine 配方时代的描述，并非对 taste 的设计意图\n说明。\nB) 与原文相反\nIt still relies on traditional kola nut extract 说仍依赖传统 kola 提取物。原文 it's said\nto no longer contain kola nut extract, relying instead on artificial imitations 直接\n相反——已不含 kola，改用人工模拟。\nC) 无中生有\nIt has become more popular among the old 说在老年人中更受欢迎。原文 about", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["A) The evolution of Coca-Cola.", "B) The success story of Coca-Cola.", "C) The medicinal value of Coca-Cola.", "D) The business strategy of Coca-Cola."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （主旨题）\n【判型】题干 What is the passage mainly about 是经典主旨题问法，问全文主要内容。\n【拆句】题干 What is the passage mainly about 结构最简单的主旨疑问句，无陷阱。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nBy the late 19th century ↔ The evolution of Coca-Cola\nYou may have heard that Coca-Cola once contained an ingredient capable of sparking particular\ndevotion in consumers: cocaine\n↔ The evolution of Coca-Cola\nit's said to no longer contain kola nut extract, relying instead on artificial imitations ↔\nThe evolution of Coca-Cola\nB/C/D 都只能覆盖文中某一面（成功/药用/商业），唯有 A 的 evolution 把从起源—传\n播—改味—今日配方的所有段落功能囊括其中。\nB) 以偏概全\nThe success story of Coca-Cola 强调\"成功故事\"。文中只 Today about 1.9 billion\nCokes are purchased daily 与 It's become so iconic 属于成功面，且 attempts to\nchange its taste in 1985 ... proved disastrous 还含明显失败叙事，不构成主旋律。\nC) 以偏概全\nThe medicinal value of Coca-Cola 把主旨锁在\"药用价值\"上。原文 Many made their\nway into medicines, intended as a kind of energy boost 讲的是 kola nuts 入药，不\n是 Coca-Cola 本身的药用价值，且只局限在 19 世纪末这一段。\nD) 以偏概全\nThe business strategy of Coca-Cola 把主旨锁到\"商业策略\"。文中仅 the company\nsold rights to bottle the soda 与 sweetening it in a move projected to boost sales\n沾点商业策略，且后者还是失败案例；文章主体仍是历史演变，不是策略剖析。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "主旨题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What do we learn about American cities twenty years ago?", "options": ["A) They were divided into residential and business areas.", "B) Their housing prices were linked with their prosperity.", "C) There was a clear divide between large and small cities.", "D) They were places where large investment capital flowed."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What do we learn about American cities twenty years ago 是对一个明确时间点的\n事实陈述发问，没有 infer/suggest/imply 一类信号，是典型的细节回指题。\n【拆句】What do we learn about American cities twenty years ago? 主句是 What do we learn\nabout X，about 后接介词宾语 American cities，twenty years ago 是修饰 American\ncities 的时间状语；结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Twenty years ago, the Urban Land Institute defined the two types of cities that dominated the\nUS landscape: smaller cities that operated around standard 9-5 business hours and large\nmetropolitan areas that ran all 24 hours of the day.\n二十年前，城市土地学会定义了主导美国格局的两类城市：按朝九晚五标准营业时间运转的\n小城市，以及全天 24 小时不停转的大都市区。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n选项把原文\"按运营时间分两类\"偷换成\"按 residential and business areas 分两类\"，城\n市内部功能分区在原文毫无出处。\nB) 强加因果\n原文只说分析两类城市能给 investment capital flows and housing prices have\nshifted 提供有趣背景，并未说 Their housing prices were linked with their\nprosperity；prosperity 这一概念在二十年前那句里并未出现。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe two types of cities that dominated the US landscape ↔\nThere was a clear divide between large and small cities.\nsmaller cities that operated around standard 9-5 business hours and large metropolitan areas that\nran all 24 hours of the day\n↔ between large and small cities\n预判落点是\"大小城市之间存在清晰划分\"，There was a clear divide between large\nand small cities. 把 two types 和 smaller cities ... large metropolitan areas 同时翻\n译过来，是定位句的同义替换，锁定 C。\nD) 以偏概全\n原文说\"用这一划分作镜头去看投资流向\"，并不等于\"美国城市是大投资流入之地\"；\nplaces where large investment capital flowed 把\"分析角度\"偷换成\"所有美国城市都\n是投资目的地\"。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What can be inferred from the passage about 18-hour cities?", "options": ["A) They especially appeal to small businesses.", "B) They have seen a rise in property prices.", "C) They have replaced quiet with excitement.", "D) They have changed America's landscape."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （推断题）\n【判型】题干 What can be inferred from the passage about 18-hour cities 明示 inferred，要求\n基于原文做一步合理延伸。\n【拆句】What can be inferred from the passage about 18-hour cities? What 作主语补语；can\nbe inferred 是被动语态，from the passage 是来源状语，about 18-hour cities 是宾语补\n足；语序常规，无陷阱。\n【定位】These 18-hour cities are beginning to make waves in real estate rankings and attract more real\nestate investment.\n这些 18 小时城市开始在房地产排行榜上崭露头角，并吸引更多的房地产投资。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文第四段说 18-hour cities 吸引 knowledge and talent industries 和\nentertainment-oriented businesses such as bars and restaurants，从未限定它\"特\n别\"吸引 small businesses，更没说\"小型企业\"是它的目标客群。\nB) ✓ 正确\nattract more real estate investment ↔ They have seen a rise in property prices.\nmake waves in real estate rankings ↔ a rise in property prices\n预判落点是\"房产升值\"，They have seen a rise in property prices. 正是这一预判的最\n佳同义改写——而且\"see + n.\"是英语里非常常见的\"经历某种变化\"的句式，与原文进行\n时 are beginning to 在时态上相容。\nC) 与原文相反\n原文是 incorporating the excitement and opportunity of large cities with small\ncities' quiet after midnight，强调\"兼具\"二者，选项 replaced quiet with excitement\n却把它扭曲成\"用兴奋取代宁静\"，方向与原文相反。\nD) 过度推断\n原文只说 18-hour cities beginning to make waves in real estate rankings，规模和\n影响都还在起步，并未到 changed America's landscape 这种全国格局被改变的程度；\n况且\"landscape 被改变\"是第一段二十年前划分的语境。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "推断题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "Years ago, many downtown cores in small to mid-sized cities__________.", "options": ["A) Had hardly any business activity", "B) Were crowded in business hours", "C) Exhibited no signs of prosperity", "D) Looked deserted in the evenings"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Years ago, many downtown cores in small to mid-sized cities__________. 是一个待\n补全的客观事实陈述句，选项是它的延续，属于细节题。\n【拆句】Years ago, many downtown cores in small to mid-sized cities__________. Years ago 作\n时间状语，主语是带介词短语修饰的 many downtown cores in small to mid-sized\ncities，谓语空缺待选项补齐；无陷阱。\n【定位】For decades, many downtown cores in small to mid-sized cities were abandoned after work\nhours by workers who lived in the suburbs.\n几十年来，许多小到中型城市的市中心在下班后被住在郊区的工作者抛弃。\n【选项】A) 以偏概全\n原文只说 This generated little commerce for downtown businesses in the\nevenings 是\"晚间\"商业少，而非\"全天 hardly any business activity\"。\nB) 无中生有\n原文未对 downtown 在工作时段的状态作任何描述，更没说它在工作时段 crowded。\nC) 过度推断\n原文虽然提到税收困难，但题干问 Years ago downtown 的整体状态，exhibited no\nsigns of prosperity 是对\"繁荣\"的全盘否定，超出原文论断。\nD) ✓ 正确\nwere abandoned after work hours ↔ looked deserted in the evenings\nby workers who lived in the suburbs ↔ looked deserted in the evenings\n预判落点是\"晚间被抛弃、冷清\"，looked deserted in the evenings 是 were\nabandoned after work hours 的近义直译，锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "What characterizes the new downtown areas in 18-hour cities?", "options": ["A) A sudden emergence of the knowledge industry.", "B) Flooding in of large crowds of migrant workers.", "C) Modernized housing and improved infrastructure.", "D) More comfortable life and greater upward mobility."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What characterizes the new downtown areas in 18-hour cities? 直接问 18-hour\ncities 新城区的特征，属于客观信息回指。\n【拆句】What characterizes the new downtown areas in 18-hour cities? What 作主语，\ncharacterizes 是单数及物动词，宾语是 the new downtown areas in 18-hour cities；\ncharacterize 意为\"成为……的特征\"，注意它是\"特征→对象\"的指向，主语是特征。\n【定位】Transforming downtown areas so that they incorporate modern housing and improved\nwalkability to local restaurants, retail, and entertainment—especially when combined\nwith improved infrastructure for cyclists and public transit—makes them appeal to a more\naffluent demographic.\n改造市中心，使其纳入现代住房并提升通往本地餐厅、零售与娱乐场所的步行便利度——尤\n其结合为骑行者与公共交通而改善的基础设施时——使它们吸引更富裕的人群。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\n原文是 encourage employers in the knowledge and talent industries to keep their\noffices downtown，强调\"留住\"既有雇主，而非 A sudden emergence of the\nknowledge industry 这种\"突然出现\"。\nB) 与原文相反\n原文吸引的是 a more affluent demographic 和 younger, creative workers and\nbaby boomers nearing retirement，从未提及 migrant workers。\nC) ✓ 正确\nmodern housing ↔ Modernized housing and improved infrastructure.\nimproved walkability to local restaurants, retail, and entertainment ↔\nimproved infrastructure\nimproved infrastructure for cyclists and public transit ↔ improved infrastructure\n预判落点是\"现代化住房 + 基础设施改善\"，Modernized housing and improved\ninfrastructure. 同时覆盖原文 modern housing 与 improved walkability ... improved\ninfrastructure，两个特征一一对应，锁定 C。\nD) 无中生有\n原文吸引的是\"已富裕人群\"，并未涉及 upward mobility（阶层向上流动）；upward\nmobility 是社会学概念，原文没有任何对应句。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2017-12-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What have 18-hour cities brought to the local residents?", "options": ["A) More chances for promotion.", "B) Healthier living environment.", "C) Greater cultural diversity.", "D) Better job opportunities."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What have 18-hour cities brought to the local residents? 直接问已经发生的结\n果，属于事实回指。\n【拆句】What have 18-hour cities brought to the local residents? What 是 brought 的宾语前\n置，have...brought 是现在完成时，to the local residents 是间接宾语；brought 用现完\n表明结果延续至今。\n【定位】In many of these cities such as Denver, a diverse and vigorous economy attracted to the urban\ncore has offered stable employment for residents.\n在许多这样的城市（比如丹佛），被吸引到市中心的多元而充满活力的经济为居民提供了稳\n定的就业。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\n原文只说提供 stable employment，没有任何关于 promotion（晋升）的描述。\nB) 过度推断\n原文未涉及任何健康/生活环境议题；Healthier living environment 与第四段的\nwalkability、cyclists 看似擦边，但原文从未把它落到\"健康\"上。\nC) 偷换概念\n原文是 a diverse and vigorous economy，diverse 修饰的是 economy（经济），而\n非选项 Greater cultural diversity 中的 culture（文化）。\nD) ✓ 正确\nhas offered stable employment for residents ↔ Better job opportunities.\n预判落点是\"稳定就业\"，Better job opportunities. 是 stable employment 的名词化同\n义改写，且其他三项都无原文出处，锁定 D。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
